module tecs.audio

--[[
Clips, voices, groups, keyed limits, and entity-owned sound.

Tecs decides what should sound and a backend makes the noise. Voice slots,
group settings, admission limits and every handle a game holds live here; the
backend under `tecs.platform.audiobackend` receives one array of commands per
frame and hands back the voices it has finished with. A machine with no output
device still gets a working object: `available` reports false, `play` answers
zero, and nothing raises.

## Playback

`load` reads a file and returns one shared clip per path. Clips shorter than
`streamSeconds` hold decoded samples that every voice reading them shares, and
longer clips stream separately for each voice. `LoadOptions.stream` overrides
that choice.

```nupp
local mixer <const> = tecs.audio.newAudio()
local step <const> = assert(mixer:load("assets/sfx/step.ogg"))
mixer:setLimit("footstep", {voices = 3, cooldown = 0.05})

local voice <const> = mixer:play(step, {gain = 0.7, group = "sfx", key = "footstep", pitchVariance = 0.1})
if voice ~= 0 then
    mixer:stop(voice, 0.2)
end
```

## Clips from memory

`loadBytes` decodes a clip from the bytes of a whole encoded file a game already
holds, in any format `load` reads from disk. A game that unpacks its own
archives hands over each file as it found it; one that holds raw samples puts a
container around them first, which for PCM is a 44-byte WAV header.

```nupp
local bytes <const> = assert(nupp.io.files.read("assets/sounds.pak")):sub(1025, 9216)
local chime <const> = assert(mixer:loadBytes("sounds.pak/chime.wav", bytes))
mixer:play(chime)
```

The name is the clip's identity exactly as a path is for `load`, and the two
share one namespace: a [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) row and a snapshot carry the
name, and loading it again returns the clip already held. A memory clip is
always resident and keeps no copy of the bytes, so it has no file for `reload`
to re-read; a game that wants new bytes loads them under a new name.

Units, in one place, because none of them is in a signature. Time is seconds
everywhere on this surface: a fade, a seek, a loop point, a cooldown, a clip's
duration. Every fade is a duration to run for rather than a moment to finish
at, so calling one twice restarts it. Gain is linear amplitude rather than
decibels, where zero is silence and one is the sound as recorded, and the level
a voice reaches the output at is its own gain times its group's gain times the
master gain. Pitch is a playback rate rather than an interval, so two is an
octave up and half as long. Position is the backend's frame rather than the
world's: the listener sits at the origin, x is positive right, y is positive
up, and z is positive behind.

A voice handle packs a slot with its generation, so a handle stays meaningful
for exactly as long as its voice sounds and never starts naming the voice that
takes the slot next. Holding a stale one is not an error and requires no
release.

## Timing on the audio clock

A command takes effect on the next buffer the backend mixes, so its timing
follows the frame rate: up to a frame late, and jittering frame by frame.
`play` (through `PlayOptions.at`), `stop`, `setGain`, `setPitch`, `setLoop`,
`setResample`, `setPosition` and `setStereo` take an optional `at` instead, in
seconds on the output clock `now` reads, and the backend splits its mix there,
so the command lands on exactly that output frame. The output clock is the
frames the mixer has rendered over `frequency`, so `now() + n / frequency()`
names the n-th frame after the last one mixed.

```nupp
local tick <const> = 1 / 50
local start <const> = mixer:now() + 0.05
for step = 0, 49 do
    mixer:setPitch(voice, 1 + step / 100, start + step * tick)
end
```

A time already mixed, zero and an omitted one all mean at once, which is the
first frame of the next buffer. Commands due on one frame apply in the order
they were sent, and one sent with no time comes after every timed one due by
then. A game sends a little ahead, as a sequencer does: a lookahead of a frame
or two absorbs an ordinary hitch and keeps every command on its frame. A time
that is not finite raises.

The clock counts frames mixed, not frames heard: it runs ahead of the speaker
by the device's buffer. It keeps running while a device drops and is reopened,
and on a void output, which keeps time at the wall clock's pace with no device
at all. It stands still only on a mixer with no clock, the silent backend a run
without the native library gets, where it reads zero and a timed command is
taken and dropped like any other.

A voice exists from the frame its start is due on, and its handle is good from
the call that returned it. Anything sent to a voice that has not started yet
lands on its first frame, after the start: a gain, a pause, a group's mute or
pause, a placement. A stop before the start cancels it, so nothing is heard and
the slot comes back.

A synthesizer and an FM chip keep clocks of their own, which their `now` reads
and their sending methods' `at` is on: the frames that voice has rendered, which
start when the voice first renders and stand still while its group is paused.
They are not this clock, and a voice's start on this one is not reported, so
music on a synthesizer and clips on the mixer are aligned by ear rather than by
arithmetic for now.

`PlayOptions.resample` and `setResample` choose how a voice reads between the
frames of its clip: `"linear"` interpolates, the default, and `"nearest"` holds
the frame its position is in, which reproduces a tracker's fixed-point mix
sample for sample.

## Groups and limits

A group name controls gain, mute, pause, resume and stop, and its settings
apply to voices that join later as well as to the ones sounding now. A key
controls admission through a concurrent voice limit and a cooldown. One voice
may carry both, and neither name has to know about the other. Reaching a limit
drops the new voice rather than stealing an older one.

## Entity-owned sound

An entity can carry [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) instead of a game keeping a
handle. The audio pass starts it, follows the writable playback fields, and
stops it when the component or the entity disappears. Write through
`world:getMut`.

## Devices and recording

`playbackDevices` and `recordingDevices` name what is attached now, and
`openMicrophone` opens one for capture. A [`Microphone`](tecs.audio.Microphone)
is pulled rather than pushed: the backend fills a bounded buffer from its own
thread and `read` empties it on the frame thread, so nothing a device thread
runs ever enters Nupp.

```nupp
for _, device in ipairs(tecs.audio.recordingDevices()) do
    print(device.id, device.name, device.frequency, device.channels)
end

local microphone <const> = assert(tecs.audio.openMicrophone({channels = 1}))
local block <const> = assert(microphone:read())
microphone:destroy()
```

Listing devices opens no mixer and no microphone, and opening a microphone opens
no mixer, so a game that only wants to name devices pays for nothing else.

## The frame

`update` is not a world system, and `install` deliberately does not add one.
Reaping voices has to continue while a world is paused, so an application
drives it from the iteration instead.

An output device that drops is reopened rather than lost. A Bluetooth headset
turning its microphone on changes the device from stereo at 48000 Hz to mono at
16000 or 24000 under a running game; a headset leaves, or comes back. The
backend notices (the stream's own error, a stream that stops asking for audio,
or a device whose format moved), opens the default output again at whatever
format it has now, and keeps the mix at its own rate and channels throughout,
resampling and folding to the device inside its callback. While no device is
open the mix keeps time without being heard, so voices that would have ended
in the gap report finished and nothing a game scheduled shifts. `available`
and `failureReason` do not move for any of this; `output` reports it, and
`update` logs each drop and each reopen under `tecs.audio`.

Only an output that can no longer reopen fails. The backend publishes a flag;
the next `update` sets `available` false, records `failureReason`, discards
queued starts and releases every active voice without replay. Destroy the mixer
and create a new one to open an output again. Starting without an output
remains a separate silent fallback, not a device-loss event.

A failed microphone keeps already buffered frames. Once drained, `read` returns
nil and the failure reason, while the value-only `readInto` raises that reason.
`failure` reports loss even before the buffer drains. `resume` refuses a failed
stream; destroy it and open a new microphone. Native capture supports one to
eight channels. The public argument range remains one to 32 for compatibility;
a valid request above eight returns an operational refusal from the native
opener. Device enumeration is snapshot-only and provides no change
notifications. Listing devices and observing failure of an active stream do not
depend on notifications.
]]

local assets = require("tecs.assets")
local audiobackend = require("tecs.platform.audiobackend")
local audiosynth = require("tecs.internal.audiosynth")
local ecs = require("tecs.ecs")
local effectspecs = require("tecs.audio.effects")
local timelines = require("tecs.audio.timeline")
local json = require("nupp.codec.json")
local log = require("nupp.log")
local random = require("nupp.random")

--- This logger name is a compatibility surface: a developer types it into a
--- level filter, so it does not move when its module does.
local LOGGER = log.named("tecs.audio")

--- The native opener each `Config.output` names.
local OPENERS: {[string]: string} = {device = "openOrVoid", void = "openVoid", memory = "openMemory"}

--- Entries the event log keeps before the oldest go.
local LOG_ENTRIES: integer = 512

--- Master's name.
local MASTER: string = "master"

--- Voices a Track holds before it steals, unless told otherwise.
local DEFAULT_POLYPHONY: integer = 8

--- Seconds a stolen voice fades over, so a steal does not click.
local STEAL_FADE: number = 0.01

--- Voices the backend holds past `maxVoices`, so a stolen voice can fade out
--- while the one that took its slot starts.
local STEAL_HEADROOM: integer = 16

--- Frames per second the mix runs at, unless configured otherwise.
local DEFAULT_FREQUENCY: integer = 48000

--- Mix channels. Two, because that is what a stereo mix needs; a mono device
--- hears the backend's fold of the pair.
local DEFAULT_CHANNELS: integer = 2

--- Voices that may sound at once. Past this, `play` declines rather than
--- stealing: a sound that stops halfway through because something else started
--- is harder to explain than one that never started.
local DEFAULT_MAX_VOICES: integer = 32

--- Seconds past which a clip streams rather than staying resident. Ten is long
--- enough that no sound effect reaches it and short enough that no piece of
--- music misses it.
local DEFAULT_STREAM_SECONDS: number = 10

--- Frames per second a microphone captures at unless configured otherwise.
local DEFAULT_CAPTURE_FREQUENCY: integer = 48000

--- Channels a microphone captures unless configured otherwise. One, because a
--- microphone is one microphone and a game that wants a stereo pair asks.
local DEFAULT_CAPTURE_CHANNELS: integer = 1

--- Channels a microphone may be asked for. The public compatibility contract
--- accepts up to 32 even though the native capture path carries fewer.
local MAX_CAPTURE_CHANNELS: integer = 32

--- Slots a voice handle reserves for its index. The rest of the handle is a
--- generation, so a handle whose slot has been finished and reused refers to
--- nothing rather than to whatever took its place.
local SLOT_SPAN: integer = 65536

--- Generations wrap here, which keeps a handle inside a signed 32-bit field
--- and so inside the `Sound` component.
local MAX_GENERATION: integer = 32767

--- A voice that has finished, as written back into `Sound.voice`. Distinct
--- from zero, which means "not started yet". Without the distinction a
--- one-shot would restart on the frame after it ended, forever.
local FINISHED: integer = -1

--- A clip's path is its identity, for the same reason an image's name is: an
--- index is assigned in load order, and load order is not something a snapshot
--- can rely on reproducing.
local clipIds: {[string]: integer} = {}

local clipPaths: {[integer]: string} = {}
local clipCount: integer = 0

--- A group's name is its identity, on the same terms as a clip's path.
local groupIds: {[string]: integer} = {}

local groupNames: {[integer]: string} = {}
local groupCount: integer = 0

--- Returns the index of a clip path, assigning one the first time it is seen.
---
--- @param path the non-empty path, which is not checked against the filesystem
--- @return an index from one up, shared by every mixer in the process and
---     meaningless in a file, which is why `Sound` serializes the path
--- @raises string when the path is empty
export function clipId(path: string): integer
    if path == "" then
        error("tecs.audio.clipId: a clip path cannot be empty", 2)
    end
    local existing = clipIds[path]
    if existing ~= nil then
        return existing
    end
    clipCount = clipCount + 1
    clipIds[path] = clipCount
    clipPaths[clipCount] = path

    return clipCount
end

--- Returns the path a clip index represents.
---
--- @param id the index, where zero is the index of no clip at all
--- @return the path, or nil for zero and for an index never handed out
export function clipPath(id: integer): string?
    return clipPaths[id]
end

--- Returns the index of a group name, assigning one the first time it is seen.
---
--- @param name the non-empty name, which needs no declaring: naming one here
---     is all it takes for the group to exist
--- @return an index from one up, shared by every mixer in the process
--- @raises string when the name is empty
export function groupId(name: string): integer
    if name == "" then
        error("tecs.audio.groupId: a group name cannot be empty", 2)
    end
    local existing = groupIds[name]
    if existing ~= nil then
        return existing
    end
    groupCount = groupCount + 1
    groupIds[name] = groupCount
    groupNames[groupCount] = name

    return groupCount
end

--- Returns the name a group index represents.
---
--- @param id the index, where zero is the index of no group at all
--- @return the name, or nil for zero and for an index never handed out
export function groupName(id: integer): string?
    return groupNames[id]
end

--- Attaches a sound to an entity.
---
--- Presence is the instruction: an entity carrying this with a loaded clip
--- starts sounding on the next audio pass and stops when the component or the
--- entity goes away. That is what makes sound an entity rather than a handle a
--- game has to remember to release, and it is why despawning something
--- mid-sound does the obvious thing.
---
--- The audio pass sends position to the backend and does nothing else with it.
--- A caller feeding world coordinates has three jobs this component does not do
--- for it: subtract whatever it decided is listening, choose a scale between
--- world units and the backend's, and decide that a screen-space sound has no
--- world position at all and leaves `spatial` false.
---
--- The pass follows `playing`, `gain`, `loop`, `pitch` and the position for as
--- long as the voice sounds, so writing any of them is enough and nothing has
--- to restart the voice. `clip` and `group` are deliberately not followed:
--- both take a stop and a fresh start to change anyway, and following them
--- would put a read and a compare on every row that never does. Moving a sound
--- into another group is setting `group` and clearing `voice`.
---
--- Disabling an entity silences its sound and re-enabling starts it again.
@derive(nupp.derive.Debug)
export struct Sound
    --- Caller-writable. Selects a clip by its `clipId` index. Zero plays
    --- nothing.
    clip: integer

    --- Caller-writable. Requests playback. This field gives an instruction
    --- rather than a report: clearing it stops the voice and setting it again
    --- starts one, including on a one-shot that has already run out.
    playing: boolean

    --- Caller-writable. Sets linear gain before group and master gain.
    gain: number

    --- Caller-writable. Repeats playback. Clearing it part way through lets
    --- the voice play out to its end rather than cutting it.
    loop: boolean

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the playback rate. One leaves it unchanged, while
    --- two plays an octave higher in half the time.
    pitch: number

    --- Caller-writable. Reads `x`, `y` and `z` when true, and mixes without a
    --- position when false.
    spatial: boolean

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the position right of the listener.
    x: number

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the position above the listener, which is the
    --- opposite sign from world Y.
    y: number

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the position behind the listener.
    z: number

    --- Caller-writable. Selects a group by its `groupId` index. Zero joins no
    --- group.
    group: integer

    --- Engine-owned. Reports the voice the audio pass assigned: zero before it
    --- starts and negative once a one-shot has finished. Ordinary game code
    --- reads it and writes only zero, which is how it asks for the sound again.
    voice: integer
end

--- The process-wide `Sound` component definition.
---
--- Its saved fields carry the clip path and the group name rather than their
--- indices, because an index belongs to the run that handed it out and a file
--- carrying one would name whatever the next run interned in its place. The
--- voice is not saved: a snapshot records that an entity has a sound, not how
--- far through it the backend had got, so it starts again.
export const SoundComponent: ecs.ComponentDefinition<Sound> = ecs.newComponent(Sound, {
    -- This persisted component name is a compatibility surface.
    name = "Sound",
    construct = function(
        clip: integer?,
        playing: boolean?,
        gain: number?,
        loop: boolean?,
        pitch: number?,
        spatial: boolean?,
        x: number?,
        y: number?,
        z: number?,
        group: integer?
    ): Sound
        return new Sound(
            clip or 0,
            playing ~= false,
            gain or 1,
            loop == true,
            pitch or 1,
            spatial == true,
            x or 0,
            y or 0,
            z or 0,
            group or 0,
            0
        )
    end,
    default = function(): Sound
        return new Sound(0, true, 1, false, 1, false, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
    end,
    save = function(value: Sound): any
        -- These saved keys are a compatibility surface: a file written by an
        -- earlier build names them, so they do not move when their module does.
        return {
            clip = clipPaths[value.clip],
            playing = value.playing,
            gain = value.gain,
            loop = value.loop,
            pitch = value.pitch,
            spatial = value.spatial,
            x = value.x,
            y = value.y,
            z = value.z,
            group = groupNames[value.group],
        }
    end,
    load = function(value: any): Sound
        local saved = value as {[string]: any}
        local path = saved.clip as string?
        local clip: integer = 0
        if path ~= nil and path ~= "" then
            clip = clipId(path)
        end
        local name = saved.group as string?
        local group: integer = 0
        if name ~= nil and name ~= "" then
            group = groupId(name)
        end

        -- A file written before `playing` existed carries no answer, and the
        -- one that matches what it recorded is that the sound was on.

        return new Sound(
            clip,
            saved.playing ~= false,
            (saved.gain as number?) or 1,
            saved.loop == true,
            (saved.pitch as number?) or 1,
            saved.spatial == true,
            (saved.x as number?) or 0,
            (saved.y as number?) or 0,
            (saved.z as number?) or 0,
            group,
            0
        )
    end,
})

--- The `Sound` component under the name the console gives it: an emitter,
--- a channel strip on an entity. A spatial row's voice sounds on a Track of
--- its own, placed from the row's position, and sent to every `ReverbZone`
--- it is inside. `SoundComponent` stays as an alias, and the component keeps
--- its persisted name, `"Sound"`.
export const TrackComponent: ecs.ComponentDefinition<Sound> = SoundComponent

--- Marks the entity the mixer listens from.
---
--- With one in the world, carrying a `Transform2D` or a `Transform3D`, the
--- audio pass places every spatial `Sound` row on an entity with a transform
--- itself: its position less the listener's, over `scale`. On a 2D
--- transform, world y grows downwards and the listener's frame upwards, so
--- the sign flips. Without one, a game sets each row's `x`, `y` and `z`, as
--- it always could. One listener is read: the first the query finds.
@derive(nupp.derive.Debug)
export struct Listener
    --- Caller-writable. World units per unit of audio distance; a sound one
    --- unit away is half as loud as at the listener. One by default.
    scale: number
end

--- The process-wide `Listener` component definition.
export const ListenerComponent: ecs.ComponentDefinition<Listener> = ecs.newComponent(Listener, {
    -- This persisted component name is a compatibility surface.
    name = "Listener",
    construct = function(scale: number?): Listener
        return new Listener(scale or 1)
    end,
    default = function(): Listener
        return new Listener(1)
    end,
    save = function(value: Listener): any
        return {scale = value.scale}
    end,
    load = function(value: any): Listener
        return new Listener(((value as {[string]: any}).scale as number?) or 1)
    end,
})

--- Places reverb in the world: every spatial `Sound` row inside `radius` of
--- the zone's transform sends to the return Bus `bus` names, at `level` at
--- the centre and falling to nothing at the edge.
---
--- The Bus is a group name, by its `groupId` index, and should carry a
--- reverb: `mixer:bus("cave", {effects = {effects.reverb({size = 0.9})}})`.
--- Zones need a `Listener`, which is what gives the rows a position.
@derive(nupp.derive.Debug)
export struct ReverbZone
    --- Caller-writable. The return Bus, by its `groupId` index.
    bus: integer

    --- Caller-writable. How far the zone reaches, in world units.
    radius: number

    --- Caller-writable. The send level at the centre, linear.
    level: number
end

--- The process-wide `ReverbZone` component definition.
export const ReverbZoneComponent: ecs.ComponentDefinition<ReverbZone> = ecs.newComponent(ReverbZone, {
    -- This persisted component name is a compatibility surface.
    name = "ReverbZone",
    construct = function(bus: integer?, radius: number?, level: number?): ReverbZone
        return new ReverbZone(bus or 0, radius or 1, level or 0.5)
    end,
    default = function(): ReverbZone
        return new ReverbZone(0, 1, 0.5)
    end,
    save = function(value: ReverbZone): any
        return {bus = groupNames[value.bus], radius = value.radius, level = value.level}
    end,
    load = function(value: any): ReverbZone
        local fields = value as {[string]: any}
        local name = fields.bus as string?

        return new ReverbZone(
            name ~= nil and groupId(name) or 0,
            (fields.radius as number?) or 1,
            (fields.level as number?) or 0.5
        )
    end,
})

--- One zone as the audio pass found it this frame, in the listener's frame.
local record Zone
    bus: string
    x: number
    y: number
    z: number
    radius: number
    level: number
end

--- One playback in progress, as this module tracks it.
local record Voice
    clip: integer

    --- What the game asked for, before the group and the master.
    gain: number

    --- The gain last sent, so an unchanged one costs no command.
    applied: number

    --- The rate last sent, for the same reason and because a reused slot would
    --- otherwise keep the previous voice's rate.
    pitch: number

    --- The resampling last sent: true holds the source frame.
    nearest: boolean

    --- The group name, or nil.
    group: string?

    --- The Track the voice sounds on, zero for Master's default Track.
    track: integer

    --- What a steal compares: a voice with a lower one goes first.
    priority: number

    --- The emitter Track a spatial `Sound` row's voice sounds on, made for it
    --- and destroyed with it.
    strip: Track?

    --- Counts starts, so a steal among equals takes the oldest.
    order: integer

    --- The limit bucket, or nil.
    key: string?

    --- Rises each time the slot is taken, so a stale handle is inert.
    generation: integer

    sounding: boolean

    --- Set while a stop is fading out. Such a voice keeps its slot until the
    --- backend says it has finished, and takes no further instruction.
    stopping: boolean

    --- Tracked here because a paused voice is not a finished one.
    paused: boolean

    --- True when a `Sound` component started this voice.
    owned: boolean

    --- Set by the pass that walks `Sound` rows, cleared before each walk.
    referenced: boolean

    loop: boolean

    --- The last position sent, so an unmoved sound costs no command. At most
    --- one of this and `stereo` is ever set: the backend holds one placement
    --- per voice.
    spatial: boolean

    x: number
    y: number
    z: number
    stereo: boolean
    left: number
    right: number
end

--- How many voices one key holds, and when one last started.
local record Bucket
    count: integer
    last: number
end

--- One entry in the mixer's event log.
export record Event
    --- Read-only. Counts entries since the mixer was made, so a reader asks
    --- for what came after the last one it saw.
    sequence: integer

    --- Read-only. Seconds on the output clock: a start's or a stop's `at` when
    --- it had one, and otherwise the clock when it was sent or reported.
    time: number

    --- Read-only. `"start"`, `"refuse"` for a start that did not happen,
    --- `"stop"`, `"finish"` for a voice that ended, and `"steal"` for one
    --- stopped to make room.
    event: "start" | "refuse" | "stop" | "finish" | "steal"

    --- Read-only. The voice, and zero for a refused start.
    handle: integer

    --- Read-only. The clip's name, when the event names one.
    sound: string?

    --- Read-only. The group the voice played in.
    group: string?

    --- Read-only. Why a start was refused: `"unavailable"`, `"limit"` or
    --- `"voices"`.
    reason: string?
end

--- What a recording of the mix sounded like, as numbers an agent can read.
export type Listening = {
    --- Read-only. Frames recorded, and the seconds they span.
    frames: integer,
    seconds: number,

    --- Read-only. Output clock seconds the recording began at.
    started: number,

    --- Read-only. Largest absolute sample, linear and in dBFS, nil for
    --- digital silence.
    peak: number,
    peakDbfs: number?,

    --- Read-only. Root mean square, linear and in dBFS.
    rms: number,
    rmsDbfs: number?,

    --- Read-only. Integrated loudness in LUFS, BS.1770 gated, nil when the
    --- recording is quieter than the absolute gate.
    lufs: number?,

    --- Read-only. Samples at or beyond full scale.
    clipped: integer,

    --- Read-only. Silent stretches, each a start and an end in seconds into
    --- the recording, at most sixteen.
    silent: {{number}},

    --- Read-only. Seconds into the recording where the level jumps, at most
    --- sixty-four.
    onsets: {number}
}

--- Counters an agent reads to see what the mixer refused or lost.
export type Stats = {
    --- Read-only. Starts refused, for any reason.
    refused: integer,

    --- Read-only. Voices stopped to make room for another.
    steals: integer,

    --- Read-only. Timed commands that reached the backend after their frame.
    late: integer,

    --- Read-only. Times the output dropped, and reopened.
    drops: integer,
    reopens: integer
}

--- Configures `newAudio`.
export type Config = {
    --- Caller-writable. Sets the frequency the mix runs at in frames per
    --- second, and defaults to 48000. It holds for the mixer's life whatever
    --- the device runs at; the backend resamples to the device.
    frequency: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the number of channels the mix carries, and
    --- defaults to two. A device with another count is folded or spread to.
    channels: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets how many voices may sound at once, and defaults
    --- to 32.
    maxVoices: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the duration in seconds past which a clip streams
    --- instead of staying resident, and defaults to ten.
    streamSeconds: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Chooses the output the native backend opens.
    --- `"device"`, the default, opens the platform's default output, and on a
    --- machine with none a void output that opens a device once one appears.
    --- `"void"` never touches a device: the mix goes nowhere and its clock
    --- moves at the wall clock's pace, so a headless run keeps time.
    --- `"memory"` never touches a device either, and its clock moves only
    --- when `step` renders it, so every run of the same calls produces the
    --- same samples: what a test, an offline render and an agent comparing two
    --- runs want. Ignored when `backend` is given.
    output: ("device" | "void" | "memory")?,

    --- Caller-writable. Voices a Track holds at once before it steals, for
    --- every Track `track` makes without its own; eight by default.
    polyphony: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. False leaves Master without the limiter it carries by
    --- default, which holds the mix under -1 dBFS.
    limiter: boolean?,

    --- Caller-writable. Supplies the backend instead of opening the native
    --- one, which is what a device-free test uses.
    backend: audiobackend.Backend?
}

--- Configures `Audio.load` and `Audio.loadBytes`.
export type LoadOptions = {
    --- Caller-writable. Forces streaming when true and residency when false.
    --- Left unset, the clip's duration decides against `streamSeconds`.
    --- `loadBytes` raises on true, because a clip from memory has no file to
    --- stream from, and is resident either way.
    stream: boolean?
}

--- Names how a voice reads between the frames of its clip.
---
--- `"linear"` interpolates between the two source frames either side of the
--- read position, which is the default. `"nearest"` holds the source frame the
--- position is in, its integer part, which is how a tracker stepping a
--- fixed-point position through its samples reads them: a voice in this mode
--- reproduces such a mix sample for sample, and a one-shot holds its last
--- frame for that frame's own span before it ends.
export type Resample = "linear" | "nearest"

--- A Sound of encoded audio, loaded through `tecs.assets`.
---
--- What `PCM` and `PCM_STREAM` produce: an immutable asset, shared by every
--- mixer that plays it. A resident one holds the file's bytes, which a mixer
--- decodes the first time it plays it; a streamed one holds only its path,
--- which the mixer reads from as it plays. A reload through
--- `Assets.reload` yields a new one, which the next `play` decodes afresh.
export record PcmSound
    --- Read-only. The path it was loaded from.
    path: string

    --- Read-only. The encoded bytes, nil for a streamed Sound.
    bytes: string?

    --- Read-only. Whether the mixer streams it from its file.
    stream: boolean

    --- Engine-owned. Tells two loads of one path apart.
    serial: integer
end

--- What `play` takes: a clip this mixer loaded, a Sound asset, or the handle
--- a Sound was requested under, which follows the handle's reloads.
export type Playable = Clip | PcmSound | assets.Handle

--- Counts PcmSounds made, so a reloaded one names a clip of its own.
local nextSerial: integer = 0

--- Makes the PcmSound a kind's job answers.
local function pcmSound(path: string, bytes: string?): PcmSound
    nextSerial = nextSerial + 1

    return new PcmSound(path = path, bytes = bytes, stream = bytes == nil, serial = nextSerial)
end

--- The asset kind that loads a Sound resident: the whole file's bytes, decoded
--- by the mixer that first plays it.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local step <const> = assert(app.assets:request(tecs.audio.PCM, "sounds/step.wav"))
--- mixer:play(step, {pitch = 1.05})
--- ```
export const PCM: assets.Kind = new assets.Kind(
    name = assets.SOUND,
    open = function(path: string): (assets.Job?, string?)
        local read, reason = assets.readJob(path)
        if read == nil then
            return nil, reason
        end
        local job = assert(read)

        return new assets.Job(
            poll = function(): (assets.Status, any?, string?)
                local status, value, failure = job.poll()
                if status == "ready" then
                    return "ready", pcmSound(path, value as string), nil
                else
                    return status, nil, failure
                end
            end,
            cancel = job.cancel
        )
    end,
    release = nil
)

--- The asset kind that streams a Sound from its file as it plays, for music
--- and anything too long to hold decoded.
export const PCM_STREAM: assets.Kind = new assets.Kind(
    name = assets.SOUND .. ".stream",
    open = function(path: string): (assets.Job?, string?)
        if path == "" then
            return nil, "a streamed sound needs a path"
        end

        return new assets.Job(
            poll = function(): (assets.Status, any?, string?)
                return "ready", pcmSound(path, nil), nil
            end,
            cancel = function(): nil
            end
        )
    end,
    release = nil
)

--- Configures `Audio.play`.
---
--- Read once, when the voice starts. Changing the record afterwards reaches
--- nothing: use the handle setters instead. Nothing here is retained, so one
--- record may be filled and reused for every play.
export type PlayOptions = {
    --- Caller-writable. The Track to sound on. Omitted, the voice sounds on
    --- its group's Bus's default Track, or Master's without a group. A Track
    --- names its Bus, so `group` is ignored beside it.
    track: Track?,

    --- Caller-writable. What a steal compares, zero by default. Over a
    --- Track's polyphony the lowest goes first, the oldest among equals; over
    --- `maxVoices` a voice with a strictly lower priority is stolen, or the
    --- start is refused.
    priority: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets linear gain before group and master gain, and
    --- defaults to one.
    gain: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Repeats playback until stopped, and defaults to false.
    loop: boolean?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the position in seconds a repeat returns to, so
    --- an intro can play once and the rest of it loop. Defaults to zero.
    loopStart: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets where the first pass begins in seconds, and
    --- defaults to zero.
    start: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the fade-in duration in seconds, and defaults to
    --- zero.
    fadeIn: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the playback rate, and defaults to one.
    pitch: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the fraction of `pitch` varied for each new
    --- voice, so 0.1 spreads voices over plus or minus a tenth. Defaults to
    --- zero.
    pitchVariance: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Selects the group this voice joins, and defaults to
    --- none.
    group: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. Selects the limit bucket this voice counts against,
    --- and defaults to none.
    key: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. Enables spatial positioning. See
    --- [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) for the coordinate system.
    spatial: boolean?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the position right of the listener.
    x: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the position above the listener.
    y: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the position behind the listener.
    z: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Pins the voice to the front pair of speakers, which is
    --- a pan rather than a position. Ignored when `spatial` is set, because
    --- the backend holds one placement per voice.
    stereo: boolean?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets left-speaker gain on the same linear scale as
    --- `gain`, and defaults to one.
    left: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets right-speaker gain on the same terms as `left`.
    right: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Selects how the voice reads between source frames,
    --- and defaults to `"linear"`.
    resample: Resample?,

    --- Caller-writable. Starts the voice at this many seconds on the output
    --- clock `Audio.now` reads, on exactly that output frame, rather than at
    --- the next buffer. Omitted, zero and a time already mixed mean at once.
    --- The handle is live from this call, but the backend has no voice under
    --- it until then: a command for it sent with no `at` before that time
    --- reaches nothing, so a game scheduling a start schedules what follows
    --- it too.
    at: number?
}

--- Defines what one key allows.
export type Limit = {
    --- Caller-writable. Sets how many voices this key may hold at once. Zero
    --- or absent removes the ceiling.
    voices: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the cooldown in seconds after a voice starts.
    --- Zero disables the cooldown.
    cooldown: number?
}

--- Reports one shared playable sound after its load.
export record Clip
    --- Read-only. Reports the path the clip was loaded from, or the name
    --- `loadBytes` was given, exactly as given. This is a clip's identity, so
    --- two spellings of one file are two clips.
    path: string

    --- Read-only. Reports the index a [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) carries.
    id: integer

    --- Read-only. Reports `"ready"`, `"failed"` or `"released"`. These words
    --- reach an agent through the debug server's clip list, so they are a
    --- compatibility surface rather than identifiers this tree renames.
    --- `"released"` means the load succeeded and the owning mixer later
    --- returned its samples.
    status: string

    --- Read-only. Reports the reason when loading failed, and nil otherwise.
    error: string?

    --- Read-only. Reports the audio duration in seconds, and zero when the
    --- input cannot say.
    duration: number

    --- Read-only. Reports whether decoded audio stays in memory. False
    --- identifies a clip each voice reads from the file for itself.
    resident: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports whether the clip was decoded from bytes `loadBytes`
    --- was given rather than read from a file. Such a clip has no file, so
    --- `reload` reports false for it.
    memory: boolean
end

--- Describes one sounding voice for inspection.
export record VoiceInfo
    --- Read-only. Reports the handle `playing` and `stop` accept.
    handle: integer

    --- Read-only. Reports the clip path, or nil after release.
    clip: string?

    --- Read-only. Reports the gain the voice requested, before group and
    --- master gain.
    gain: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the gain sent for the voice, group gain and group
    --- mute included. Master gain is not in it: that is one number on the
    --- output rather than something multiplied per voice.
    applied: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the playback rate sent, so the value already
    --- includes whatever variance `play` drew.
    pitch: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the group name, or nil for no group.
    group: string?

    --- Read-only. Reports the limit bucket, or nil for no bucket.
    key: string?

    --- Read-only. Reports whether its own pause or its group holds it.
    paused: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports whether a fade-out is stopping the voice.
    stopping: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports whether a [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) component
    --- started the voice instead of `play`.
    owned: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports whether playback repeats at the end.
    loop: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports whether the voice carries a position. This stays
    --- false when `stereo` does not, because a voice holds one placement.
    spatial: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports the last horizontal position sent, positive right.
    --- It has no meaning unless `spatial`.
    x: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the last vertical position sent, positive up. It has
    --- no meaning unless `spatial`.
    y: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the last depth position sent, positive behind. It
    --- has no meaning unless `spatial`.
    z: number

    --- Read-only. Reports whether the voice is pinned to the front pair.
    stereo: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports the last left-speaker gain sent. It has no meaning
    --- unless `stereo`.
    left: number

    --- Read-only. Reports the last right-speaker gain sent. It has no meaning
    --- unless `stereo`.
    right: number
end

--- Names one physical audio device.
---
--- Re-exported from the backend contract so a game reaches it as
--- `tecs.audio.Device`.
export type Device = audiobackend.Device

--- Configures `openMicrophone`.
export type MicrophoneConfig = {
    --- Caller-writable. Selects a physical device by the `id` a
    --- [`Device`](tecs.audio.Device) from `recordingDevices` reported. Omitted
    --- or zero uses the system's current default. An id names a position in
    --- the listing that produced it, so a game keeping a choice across runs
    --- keeps `deviceName` instead.
    device: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Selects a physical device by the `name` a
    --- [`Device`](tecs.audio.Device) reported, and wins over `device`. This is
    --- the durable way to name a device, because a name survives a run and an
    --- id moves when something is attached or removed.
    deviceName: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the frames per second `read` answers in, and
    --- defaults to 48000. The backend converts the device's own rate, so this
    --- is what arrives rather than what the hardware runs at.
    frequency: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the interleaved channels `read` answers with, and
    --- defaults to one. The backend folds or spreads the device's own channels
    --- to reach it. The native backend carries at most eight, so a count above
    --- that and inside the accepted range is refused with a reason rather than
    --- raised.
    channels: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets how many frames the capture holds before the
    --- oldest are dropped, and defaults to one second at `frequency`.
    bufferFrames: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Supplies the device provider instead of resolving the
    --- native one, which is what a device-free test uses.
    devices: audiobackend.Devices?
}

--- An open recording device, pulled rather than pushed.
---
--- Capture is running by the time one of these exists and the backend's own
--- thread is filling a buffer behind it. Nothing arrives until `read` asks, and
--- nothing that thread runs enters Nupp: a device thread is one the Lua virtual
--- machine never created, so the frames cross by this side emptying a buffer
--- rather than by anything calling in.
---
--- The buffer is bounded. When a game stops reading, capture drops the oldest
--- frames and counts them in `overruns`, preserving the most recent audio
--- without unbounded memory or latency growth.
export record Microphone
    --- Read-only. Reports the frames per second `read` answers in. This is the
    --- requested value, not the device's own.
    frequency: integer

    --- Read-only. Reports the interleaved channels per frame `read` answers
    --- with. This is the requested value, not the device's own.
    channels: integer

    @private capture: audiobackend.Capture?

    --- Read-only. Reports terminal capture failure, or nil while the stream is
    --- healthy. Frames already buffered remain readable; after draining, `read`
    --- returns nil and this reason, and `readInto` raises it. A zero-frame read
    --- remains a no-op. Recovery requires closing and reopening the microphone.
    failure: function(borrows self: Microphone): string?

    --- Read-only. Reports the complete sample frames ready to read without
    --- waiting, so a partly arrived frame is not counted. Zero once destroyed,
    --- rather than an error.
    availableFrames: function(borrows self: Microphone): integer

    --- Read-only. Reports the frames dropped because nothing read them in time,
    --- counted since the microphone opened. Zero once destroyed.
    overruns: function(borrows self: Microphone): integer

    --- Read-only. Pulls up to `maxFrames` complete frames as interleaved
    --- native-endian float32 samples, `channels * 4` bytes per frame. Omitting
    --- `maxFrames` takes everything ready now, and a limit above what is ready
    --- takes what is ready rather than waiting. An empty string means nothing
    --- was ready and is not a failure. Returns nil and the reason for a
    --- destroyed microphone or a backend failure, and raises when `maxFrames`
    --- is not a non-negative integer.
    read: function(exclusive self: Microphone, maxFrames: integer?): (string?, string?)

    --- Read-only. Pulls up to `maxFrames` complete frames into `out` as
    --- interleaved samples from index one, writing no more frames than `out`
    --- holds, and returns how many it wrote. This is the allocation-free form
    --- of `read`, for a game draining a microphone every frame.
    readInto: function(exclusive self: Microphone, out: {number}, maxFrames: integer?): integer

    --- Read-only. Stops the device filling the buffer, keeping whatever is
    --- already in it. Pausing one already paused succeeds; a destroyed
    --- microphone reports false with a reason.
    pause: function(exclusive self: Microphone): (boolean, string?)

    --- Read-only. Starts the device filling the buffer again after `pause`. A
    --- destroyed microphone reports false with a reason.
    resume: function(exclusive self: Microphone): (boolean, string?)

    --- Read-only. Stops capture and closes the recording device. Safe more than
    --- once. Whatever was captured and not yet read is discarded with the
    --- buffer, so a last `read` belongs before this rather than after.
    destroy: function(exclusive self: Microphone): nil
end

--- One output and the voices sounding on it.
---
--- Nothing here is thread safe and nothing here calls back. Every method runs
--- on the thread that calls it, and the backend's own audio thread reaches
--- Nupp only by leaving finished handles where `update` collects them.
export record Audio
    --- Read-only. Reports terminal output loss observed by `update`. Nil until
    --- the output can no longer reopen a device. Loss clears voices, prevents
    --- playback and requires a new mixer. A device that drops and is reopened
    --- is not a loss and leaves this nil; `output` reports it.
    failureReason: string?

    @private closed: boolean

    --- Read-only. Reports whether an output opened and remains usable as of
    --- the last `update`. False also describes a device-free mixer.
    available: boolean

    @private backend: audiobackend.Backend
    @private streamSeconds: number
    @private voices: {Voice}
    @private free: {integer}
    @private live: integer
    @private master: number
    @private mutedFlag: boolean
    @private groupGains: {[string]: number}
    @private groupMutedFlags: {[string]: boolean}
    @private groupPausedFlags: {[string]: boolean}
    @private limits: {[string]: Limit}
    @private buckets: {[string]: Bucket}
    @private clipsById: {[integer]: Clip}
    @private variance: random.Xoshiro128
    @private elapsed: number
    @private pending: {audiobackend.Command}
    @private pendingCount: integer
    @private finished: {integer}
    @private playSystem: ecs.System?
    @private outputChanges: integer
    @private outputDrops: integer
    @private outputReopens: integer
    @private history: {Event}
    @private sequence: integer
    @private refusals: integer
    @private stolen: integer
    @private stepRemainder: number
    @private held: boolean
    @private touched: boolean
    @private busByName: {[string]: Bus}
    @private busById: {[integer]: Bus}
    @private trackById: {[integer]: Track}
    @private nextBus: integer
    @private nextTrack: integer
    @private nextEffect: integer
    @private nextOrder: integer
    @private defaultPolyphony: integer
    @private clockline: timelines.Timeline?
    @private registry: integer
    @private soundClips: {[PcmSound]: Clip}
    @private zones: {Zone}
    @private timelineList: {timelines.Timeline}

    flush: function(exclusive self: Audio): nil
    now: function(borrows self: Audio): number
    frequency: function(borrows self: Audio): integer
    destroy: function(exclusive self: Audio): nil
    decoders: function(borrows self: Audio): {string}
    output: function(borrows self: Audio): audiobackend.OutputStatus
    load: function(exclusive self: Audio, path: string, options: LoadOptions?): (Clip?, string?)
    loadBytes: function(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, bytes: string, options: LoadOptions?): (Clip?, string?)
    clip: function(borrows self: Audio, id: integer): Clip?
    clips: function(borrows self: Audio): {Clip}
    reload: function(exclusive self: Audio, path: string): (boolean, string?)
    play: function(exclusive self: Audio, sound: Playable?, options: PlayOptions?): integer
    stop: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, fadeOut: number?, at: number?): nil
    stopAll: function(exclusive self: Audio, fadeOut: number?): nil
    playing: function(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): boolean
    paused: function(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): boolean
    pause: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): nil
    resume: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): nil
    setGain: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, gain: number, at: number?): nil
    setPitch: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, ratio: number, at: number?): nil
    setLoop: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, loop: boolean, at: number?): nil
    setResample: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, mode: Resample, at: number?): nil
    resample: function(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): Resample?
    looping: function(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): boolean
    seek: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, seconds: number): boolean
    tell: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): number?
    setPosition: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, x: number, y: number, z: number, at: number?): nil
    setStereo: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, left: number, right: number, at: number?): nil
    clearSpatial: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): nil
    setMasterGain: function(exclusive self: Audio, gain: number): nil
    masterGain: function(borrows self: Audio): number
    setMuted: function(exclusive self: Audio, muted: boolean): nil
    muted: function(borrows self: Audio): boolean
    sounding: function(borrows self: Audio): integer
    maxVoices: function(borrows self: Audio): integer
    setGroupGain: function(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, gain: number): nil
    groupGain: function(borrows self: Audio, name: string): number
    setGroupMuted: function(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, muted: boolean): nil
    groupMuted: function(borrows self: Audio, name: string): boolean
    pauseGroup: function(exclusive self: Audio, name: string): nil
    resumeGroup: function(exclusive self: Audio, name: string): nil
    groupPaused: function(borrows self: Audio, name: string): boolean
    groups: function(borrows self: Audio): {string}
    stopGroup: function(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, fadeOut: number?): nil
    setLimit: function(exclusive self: Audio, key: string, limit: Limit?): nil
    limit: function(borrows self: Audio, key: string): Limit?
    keyCount: function(borrows self: Audio, key: string): integer
    keys: function(borrows self: Audio): {string}
    voiceList: function(borrows self: Audio): {VoiceInfo}
    update: function(exclusive self: Audio, dt: number?): integer
    step: function(exclusive self: Audio, seconds: number): number
    hold: function(exclusive self: Audio, held: boolean): boolean
    tick: function(exclusive self: Audio, dt: number): nil
    listen: function(exclusive self: Audio, seconds: number): boolean
    listening: function(borrows self: Audio): Listening?
    saveListening: function(borrows self: Audio, path: string): (boolean, string?)
    stopListening: function(exclusive self: Audio): nil
    events: function(borrows self: Audio, after: integer?): {Event}
    stats: function(borrows self: Audio): Stats
    bus: function(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, options: BusOptions?): Bus
    masterBus: function(exclusive self: Audio): Bus
    buses: function(borrows self: Audio): {Bus}
    track: function(exclusive self: Audio, options: TrackOptions?): Track
    tracks: function(borrows self: Audio): {Track}
    meters: function(exclusive self: Audio): Meters
    timeline: function(exclusive self: Audio, options: timelines.Options?): timelines.Timeline
    defaultTimeline: function(exclusive self: Audio): timelines.Timeline
    timelines: function(borrows self: Audio): {timelines.Timeline}
    registerDecoder: function(exclusive self: Audio, vtable: any): (integer?, string?)
    registerEffect: function(exclusive self: Audio, vtable: any): (integer?, string?)
    playDecoded: function(
        exclusive self: Audio,
        decoder: integer,
        params: string,
        bytes: string,
        options: PlayOptions?
    ): (integer, string?)
    event: function(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, data: integer, at: number?): boolean
end

--- Options for `Audio.bus`.
export type BusOptions = {
    --- Caller-writable. The Bus this one sums into, Master when omitted.
    parent: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. The fader, one by default.
    gain: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Starts it muted.
    muted: boolean?,

    --- Caller-writable. Its insert chain, in order.
    effects: {effectspecs.Spec}?
}

--- Options for `Audio.track`.
export type TrackOptions = {
    --- Caller-writable. The Bus it routes to, Master when omitted.
    bus: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. The fader, one by default.
    gain: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Starts it muted, or soloed.
    muted: boolean?,
    solo: boolean?,

    --- Caller-writable. Places its sum against the listener at `x y z`.
    spatial: boolean?,
    x: number?,
    y: number?,
    z: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Post-fader sends, by return Bus name and level.
    sends: {[string]: number}?,

    --- Caller-writable. Voices it holds at once before it steals, the
    --- mixer's `polyphony` (eight) by default.
    polyphony: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Its insert chain, in order.
    effects: {effectspecs.Spec}?
}

--- One effect in a strip's insert chain.
export record Effect
    --- Read-only. The id the mixer gave it.
    id: integer

    --- Read-only. Its kind's name.
    name: string

    @private owner: integer
    @private names: {[string]: integer}
    @private values: {[integer]: number}
    @private removed: boolean
    @private onBus: boolean
    @private strip: integer

    set: function(exclusive self: Effect, name: string, value: number, at: number?): nil
    get: function(borrows self: Effect, name: string): number?
    remove: function(exclusive self: Effect): nil
end

--- A subgroup, a return, or Master: sums what routes to it, through its
--- inserts and fader, into its parent.
export record Bus
    --- Read-only. Its name; Master's is `"master"`.
    name: string

    --- Read-only. The id the backend knows it by; Master's is zero.
    id: integer

    @private owner: integer
    @private parentName: string?
    @private level: number
    @private mutedFlag: boolean
    @private pausedFlag: boolean
    @private home: Track?
    @private chain: {Effect}
    @private destroyed: boolean

    setGain: function(exclusive self: Bus, gain: number, at: number?): nil
    gain: function(borrows self: Bus): number
    setMuted: function(exclusive self: Bus, muted: boolean, at: number?): nil
    muted: function(borrows self: Bus): boolean
    pause: function(exclusive self: Bus, at: number?): nil
    resume: function(exclusive self: Bus, at: number?): nil
    paused: function(borrows self: Bus): boolean
    held: function(borrows self: Bus): boolean
    parent: function(borrows self: Bus): string?
    setParent: function(exclusive self: Bus, name: string?): nil
    track: function(exclusive self: Bus): Track
    insert: function(exclusive self: Bus, spec: effectspecs.Spec, position: integer?): Effect
    effects: function(borrows self: Bus): {Effect}
    destroy: function(exclusive self: Bus): nil
end

--- A channel strip: voices sum into it, through its inserts, fader and pan
--- or position, into its Bus and its sends.
export record Track
    --- Read-only. The id the backend knows it by; Master's default Track is
    --- zero.
    id: integer

    @private owner: integer
    @private busName: string
    @private level: number
    @private mutedFlag: boolean
    @private soloFlag: boolean
    @private placed: "none" | "spatial" | "stereo"
    @private px: number
    @private py: number
    @private pz: number
    @private sendLevels: {[string]: number}
    @private limit: integer
    @private chain: {Effect}
    @private isDefault: boolean
    @private destroyed: boolean

    play: function(exclusive self: Track, sound: Playable?, options: PlayOptions?): integer
    stopAll: function(exclusive self: Track, fadeOut: number?): nil
    sounding: function(borrows self: Track): integer
    setGain: function(exclusive self: Track, gain: number, at: number?): nil
    gain: function(borrows self: Track): number
    setMuted: function(exclusive self: Track, muted: boolean, at: number?): nil
    muted: function(borrows self: Track): boolean
    setSolo: function(exclusive self: Track, solo: boolean, at: number?): nil
    solo: function(borrows self: Track): boolean
    bus: function(borrows self: Track): string
    setBus: function(exclusive self: Track, name: string): nil
    setPosition: function(exclusive self: Track, x: number, y: number, z: number, at: number?): nil
    setStereo: function(exclusive self: Track, left: number, right: number, at: number?): nil
    clearPlacement: function(exclusive self: Track, at: number?): nil
    position: function(borrows self: Track): (number?, number?, number?)
    send: function(exclusive self: Track, bus: string, level: number, at: number?): nil
    sends: function(borrows self: Track): {[string]: number}
    polyphony: function(borrows self: Track): integer
    setPolyphony: function(exclusive self: Track, voices: integer): nil
    insert: function(exclusive self: Track, spec: effectspecs.Spec, position: integer?): Effect
    effects: function(borrows self: Track): {Effect}
    destroy: function(exclusive self: Track): nil
end

--- What `Audio.meters` reports for one strip since the last read.
export type Meter = {
    --- Read-only. Largest absolute sample, linear.
    peak: number,

    --- Read-only. Root mean square, linear.
    rms: number,

    --- Read-only. Samples at or past full scale.
    clipped: integer
}

--- Every strip's meter: Master, each Bus by name, each Track by id.
export type Meters = {
    master: Meter,
    buses: {[string]: Meter},
    tracks: {[integer]: Meter}
}

--- Every live mixer by the id its Buses, Tracks and Effects name it by,
--- weakly, so a mixer that has gone takes its entry with it.
local mixers: {[integer]: Audio} = setmetatable({}, {__mode = "v"})

local nextMixer: integer = 0

--- Returns the mixer a console object belongs to.
---
--- @raises string when the mixer has been collected
local function mixerOf(id: integer): Audio
    return assert(mixers[id], "tecs.audio: the mixer this belongs to is gone")
end

--- The mixer each world had installed into it, keyed weakly so a world that
--- has gone takes its entry with it.
local installed: {[ecs.World]: Audio} = setmetatable({}, {__mode = "k"})

--- Returns the next command record, growing the buffer only when a frame has
--- never needed this many. Steady state allocates nothing.
local function queue(exclusive self: Audio, kind: integer, handle: integer): audiobackend.Command
    local index = self.pendingCount + 1
    self.pendingCount = index
    local command = self.pending[index]
    if command == nil then
        command = audiobackend.newCommand()
        self.pending[index] = command
    end
    command.kind = kind
    command.handle = handle
    command.clip = 0
    command.flags = 0
    command.gain = 1
    command.pitch = 1
    command.start = 0
    command.fade = 0
    command.loopStart = 0
    command.x = 0
    command.y = 0
    command.z = 0
    command.at = 0

    return command
end

--- Reports whether a call carries a time on the output clock, which is what
--- makes it bypass the skip for an unchanged value: a timed value may land
--- before or after one already sent, so only the backend knows which is last.
local function isTimed(at: number?): boolean
    return at ~= nil and at > 0
end

--- Raises for an `at` that names no time: not a number, or not finite.
---
--- A time already mixed is a time, and means at once; one that can never
--- come is a mistake in the caller's arithmetic, typically a count of frames
--- over a frequency of zero, and waiting for it forever would hide it.
---
--- @raises string when `at` is NaN or infinite
local function checkAt(at: number?, method: string): nil
    if at == nil then
        return
    end
    if at ~= at or at == math.huge or at == -math.huge then
        error("tecs.audio.Audio." .. method .. ": at must be a finite time, got " .. tostring(at), 3)
    end
end

--- Returns the slot a handle names, with the generation divided back out.
local function slotOf(handle: integer): integer
    return handle - (handle // SLOT_SPAN) * SLOT_SPAN
end

--- Returns the voice a handle names, or nil when it names one that has ended.
local function voiceOf(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): Voice?
    if handle <= 0 then
        return nil
    end
    local slot = slotOf(handle)
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if voice == nil or not voice.sounding then
        return nil
    end
    if voice.generation ~= (handle - slot) // SLOT_SPAN then
        return nil
    end

    return voice
end

--- Returns the handle naming one slot's current voice.
local function handleOf(borrows self: Audio, slot: integer): integer
    return self.voices[slot].generation * SLOT_SPAN + slot
end

--- Returns the gain a voice should carry: its own. Its group's gain and mute
--- are its Bus's fader, and the master is one number on the output.
local function composedGain(borrows _self: Audio, voice: Voice): number
    -- A group is a Bus now, whose fader applies on the backend.
    return voice.gain
end

--- Sends a voice's gain, skipping the command when nothing changed.
local function applyGain(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer, at: number?): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    local applied = composedGain(self, voice)
    if applied == voice.applied and not isTimed(at) then
        return
    end
    voice.applied = applied
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.SET_GAIN, handleOf(self, slot))
    command.gain = applied
    command.at = at or 0
end

--- Sends a voice's rate, skipping the command when nothing changed.
local function applyPitch(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer, ratio: number, at: number?): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if voice.pitch == ratio and not isTimed(at) then
        return
    end
    voice.pitch = ratio
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.SET_PITCH, handleOf(self, slot))
    command.pitch = ratio
    command.at = at or 0
end

--- Sends how a voice resamples, skipping the command when nothing changed.
local function applyResample(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer, nearest: boolean, at: number?): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if voice.nearest == nearest and not isTimed(at) then
        return
    end
    voice.nearest = nearest
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.SET_RESAMPLE, handleOf(self, slot))
    command.flags = nearest and audiobackend.NEAREST or 0
    command.at = at or 0
end

--- Sends whether a voice repeats, skipping the command when nothing changed.
local function applyLoop(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer, loop: boolean, at: number?): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if voice.loop == loop and not isTimed(at) then
        return
    end
    voice.loop = loop
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.SET_LOOP, handleOf(self, slot))
    command.flags = loop and audiobackend.LOOP or 0
    command.at = at or 0
end

--- Places a voice, skipping the command when it has not moved.
local function applyPosition(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer, x: number, y: number, z: number, at: number?): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if voice.spatial and voice.x == x and voice.y == y and voice.z == z and not isTimed(at) then
        return
    end
    -- A voice holds one placement, so this leaves the other.
    voice.spatial = true
    voice.stereo = false
    voice.x = x
    voice.y = y
    voice.z = z
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.SET_POSITION, handleOf(self, slot))
    command.flags = audiobackend.SPATIAL
    command.x = x
    command.y = y
    command.z = z
    command.at = at or 0
end

--- Pans a voice, skipping the command when nothing changed.
local function applyStereo(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer, left: number, right: number, at: number?): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if voice.stereo and voice.left == left and voice.right == right and not isTimed(at) then
        return
    end
    voice.stereo = true
    voice.spatial = false
    voice.left = left
    voice.right = right
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.SET_STEREO, handleOf(self, slot))
    command.flags = audiobackend.STEREO
    command.x = left
    command.y = right
    command.at = at or 0
end

--- Returns a voice to unpositioned mixing, out of either placement.
local function applyUnplaced(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if not voice.spatial and not voice.stereo then
        return
    end
    voice.spatial = false
    voice.stereo = false
    queue(self, audiobackend.CLEAR_PLACEMENT, handleOf(self, slot))
end

--- Gives a slot back, un-counting whatever it held.
local function release(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    if not voice.sounding then
        return
    end
    local key = voice.key
    if key ~= nil then
        local bucket = self.buckets[key]
        if bucket ~= nil then
            bucket.count = bucket.count - 1
        end
    end
    voice.group = nil
    voice.key = nil
    voice.sounding = false
    voice.stopping = false
    voice.paused = false
    voice.owned = false
    voice.clip = 0
    voice.applied = -1
    voice.spatial = false
    voice.stereo = false
    self.live = self.live - 1
    self.free[#self.free + 1] = slot
    local strip = voice.strip
    if strip ~= nil then
        voice.strip = nil
        strip:destroy()
    end
end

--- Reports whether a key has room for another voice right now.
local function admits(borrows self: Audio, key: string): boolean
    local limit = self.limits[key]
    if limit == nil then
        return true
    end
    local bucket = self.buckets[key]
    if bucket == nil then
        return true
    end
    local cooldown = limit.cooldown
    if cooldown ~= nil and cooldown > 0 and self.elapsed - bucket.last < cooldown then
        return false
    end
    local ceiling = limit.voices
    if ceiling ~= nil and ceiling > 0 and bucket.count >= ceiling then
        return false
    end

    return true
end

--- Appends one entry to the event log, dropping the oldest past the cap.
local function record(
    exclusive self: Audio,
    event: "start" | "refuse" | "stop" | "finish" | "steal",
    handle: integer,
    at: number?,
    clip: integer,
    group: string?,
    reason: string?
): nil
    local sequence = self.sequence + 1
    self.sequence = sequence
    local time = (at ~= nil and at > 0) and at or self:now()
    local known = self.clipsById[clip]
    local entry = new Event(
        sequence = sequence,
        time = time,
        event = event,
        handle = handle,
        sound = known and known.path or nil,
        group = group,
        reason = reason
    )
    local history = self.history
    history[#history + 1] = entry
    if #history > LOG_ENTRIES then
        table.remove(history, 1)
    end
end

--- Counts the voices sounding on a Track, a fading one not included.
local function trackSounding(borrows self: Audio, track: integer): integer
    local count: integer = 0
    for slot = 1, #self.voices do
        local voice = self.voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding and not voice.stopping and voice.track == track then
            count = count + 1
        end
    end

    return count
end

--- Returns the slot of the voice a steal takes, or zero for none: the lowest
--- priority, the oldest among equals, on `track` or anywhere when nil, and
--- below `priority`, strictly so when `strictly`.
local function lowest(borrows self: Audio, track: integer?, priority: number, strictly: boolean): integer
    local chosen: integer = 0
    local floor = math.huge
    local oldest = math.huge
    for slot = 1, #self.voices do
        local voice = self.voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding and not voice.stopping and (track == nil or voice.track == track) then
            local eligible = strictly and voice.priority < priority or (not strictly and voice.priority <= priority)
            if eligible and (voice.priority < floor or (voice.priority == floor and voice.order < oldest)) then
                chosen = slot
                floor = voice.priority
                oldest = voice.order
            end
        end
    end

    return chosen
end

--- Stops a voice to make room, with a short fade, and frees its slot now.
local function steal(exclusive self: Audio, slot: integer): nil
    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    local handle = handleOf(self, slot)
    self.stolen = self.stolen + 1
    record(self, "steal", handle, nil, voice.clip, voice.group, nil)
    queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handle).fade = STEAL_FADE
    release(self, slot)
end

--- Counts and logs a start that did not happen, and answers its zero handle.
local function refuse(exclusive self: Audio, clip: integer, options: PlayOptions, reason: string): integer
    self.refusals = self.refusals + 1
    record(self, "refuse", 0, options.at, clip, options.group, reason)

    return 0
end

--- Takes a free slot for one voice reading `clip`, queues its start, and
--- returns its handle, or zero when nothing started.
---
--- `flags` carries what the input adds to the command: `STREAM` for a clip read
--- from its file, `SYNTH` for a synthesizer, which reads no clip at all.
local function claim(
    exclusive self: Audio,
    clip: integer,
    flags: integer,
    options: PlayOptions,
    owned: boolean
): integer
    if not self.available then
        return refuse(self, clip, options, "unavailable")
    end
    local key = options.key
    if key ~= nil and not admits(self, key) then
        return refuse(self, clip, options, "limit")
    end
    local trackId: integer = 0
    local group = options.group
    local chosen = options.track
    if chosen ~= nil then
        if chosen.destroyed then
            return refuse(self, clip, options, "track")
        end
        trackId = chosen.id
        group = chosen.busName ~= MASTER and chosen.busName or nil
    elseif group ~= nil then
        local bus = self:bus(group)
        trackId = bus:track().id
    end
    local priority = options.priority or 0
    local owner = self.trackById[trackId]
    if owner ~= nil and owner.limit > 0 and trackSounding(self, trackId) >= owner.limit then
        local victim = lowest(self, trackId, priority, false)
        if victim == 0 then
            return refuse(self, clip, options, "polyphony")
        end
        steal(self, victim)
    end
    if #self.free == 0 then
        local victim = lowest(self, nil, priority, true)
        if victim == 0 then
            return refuse(self, clip, options, "voices")
        end
        steal(self, victim)
    end
    local count = #self.free
    local slot = self.free[count]
    self.free[count] = nil

    local voice = self.voices[slot]
    voice.generation = voice.generation + 1
    if voice.generation > MAX_GENERATION then
        voice.generation = 1
    end
    voice.clip = clip
    voice.gain = options.gain or 1
    voice.applied = -1
    voice.group = group
    voice.track = trackId
    voice.priority = priority
    self.nextOrder = self.nextOrder + 1
    voice.order = self.nextOrder
    voice.key = key
    voice.sounding = true
    voice.stopping = false
    voice.paused = false
    voice.owned = owned
    voice.referenced = false
    voice.loop = options.loop == true
    voice.nearest = options.resample == "nearest"
    voice.spatial = false
    voice.stereo = false

    local pitch = options.pitch or 1
    local spread = options.pitchVariance or 0
    if spread ~= 0 then
        pitch = pitch * (1 + (self.variance:next() * 2 - 1) * spread)
    end
    voice.pitch = pitch

    local handle = handleOf(self, slot)
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.PLAY, handle)
    command.clip = clip
    command.gain = composedGain(self, voice)
    command.pitch = pitch
    command.start = options.start or 0
    command.fade = options.fadeIn or 0
    command.loopStart = options.loopStart or 0
    local at = options.at or 0
    command.at = at
    command.target = trackId
    if voice.loop then
        flags = flags + audiobackend.LOOP
    end
    if voice.nearest then
        flags = flags + audiobackend.NEAREST
    end

    -- The two placements are exclusive, and a position wins when a caller asks
    -- for both rather than one silently undoing the other.
    if options.spatial == true then
        flags = flags + audiobackend.SPATIAL
        voice.spatial = true
        voice.x = options.x or 0
        voice.y = options.y or 0
        voice.z = options.z or 0
        command.x = voice.x
        command.y = voice.y
        command.z = voice.z
    elseif options.stereo == true then
        flags = flags + audiobackend.STEREO
        voice.stereo = true
        voice.left = options.left or 1
        voice.right = options.right or 1
        command.x = voice.left
        command.y = voice.right
    end
    command.flags = flags
    voice.applied = command.gain

    if key ~= nil then
        local bucket = self.buckets[key]
        if bucket == nil then
            bucket = new Bucket(count = 0, last = 0)
            self.buckets[key] = bucket
        end
        bucket.count = bucket.count + 1
        bucket.last = self.elapsed
    end

    self.live = self.live + 1
    record(self, "start", handle, at, clip, voice.group, nil)

    return handle
end

--- Returns the clip a Playable plays as on this mixer: a clip as it is, a
--- Sound decoded or opened the first time, a handle's current Sound.
local function clipOf(exclusive self: Audio, sound: Playable?): Clip?
    local found: any = sound
    if found is assets.Handle then
        found = found:value()
    end
    if found is PcmSound then
        local known = self.soundClips[found]
        if known ~= nil then
            return known
        end
        local clip: Clip? = nil
        local bytes = found.bytes
        if bytes ~= nil then
            clip = self:loadBytes(found.path .. "#" .. tostring(found.serial), bytes)
        else
            clip = self:load(found.path, {stream = true})
        end
        if clip ~= nil then
            self.soundClips[found] = clip
        end

        return clip
    end
    if found is Clip then
        return found
    end

    return nil
end

--- Starts a clip on a free slot and returns its handle, or zero.
local function start(exclusive self: Audio, clip: Clip?, options: PlayOptions, owned: boolean): integer
    if clip == nil or clip.status ~= "ready" then
        return 0
    end

    return claim(self, clip.id, clip.resident and 0 or audiobackend.STREAM, options, owned)
end

--- Makes a Bus handle without telling the backend, which `Audio.bus` does.
local function newBus(borrows self: Audio, name: string, id: integer, parent: string?): Bus
    return new Bus(
        name = name,
        id = id,
        owner = self.registry,
        parentName = parent,
        level = 1,
        mutedFlag = false,
        pausedFlag = false,
        home = nil,
        chain = {},
        destroyed = false
    )
end

--- Makes a Track handle without telling the backend.
local function newTrack(borrows self: Audio, id: integer, bus: string, limit: integer): Track
    return new Track(
        id = id,
        owner = self.registry,
        busName = bus,
        level = 1,
        mutedFlag = false,
        soloFlag = false,
        placed = "none",
        px = 0,
        py = 0,
        pz = 0,
        sendLevels = {},
        limit = limit,
        chain = {},
        isDefault = false,
        destroyed = false
    )
end

--- Queues a strip command with its target, control and value.
local function stripCommand(
    exclusive self: Audio,
    kind: integer,
    target: integer,
    param: integer,
    value: number,
    at: number?
): audiobackend.Command
    local command = queue(self, kind, 0)
    command.target = target
    command.param = param
    command.gain = value
    command.at = at or 0

    return command
end

--- Builds an effect on the backend and inserts it into a strip's chain.
---
--- @raises string when the backend cannot build it
local function insertEffect(
    exclusive self: Audio,
    chain: {Effect},
    onBus: boolean,
    strip: integer,
    spec: effectspecs.Spec,
    position: integer?
): Effect
    local id = self.nextEffect
    self.nextEffect = id + 1
    local prepare = self.backend.prepareEffect
    if prepare ~= nil and not self.closed then
        local ok, reason = prepare(spec.kind, id, spec.params)
        if not ok then
            error("tecs.audio: the effect " .. spec.name .. " was not built: " .. tostring(reason), 3)
        end
    end
    local at = position or (#chain + 1)
    if at < 1 then
        at = 1
    elseif at > #chain + 1 then
        at = #chain + 1
    end
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.EFFECT_INSERT, 0)
    command.target = strip
    command.flags = onBus and audiobackend.ON_BUS or 0
    command.param = at - 1
    command.data = id
    local values: {[integer]: number} = {}
    for param, value in pairs(spec.values) do
        values[param] = value
        local set = queue(self, audiobackend.EFFECT_PARAM, 0)
        set.data = id
        set.param = param
        set.gain = value
    end
    local effect = new Effect(
        id = id,
        name = spec.name,
        owner = self.registry,
        names = spec.names,
        values = values,
        removed = false,
        onBus = onBus,
        strip = strip
    )
    table.insert(chain, at, effect)

    return effect
end

--- Raises unless `value` is a finite number.
---
--- @raises string when it is NaN or infinite
local function checkFinite(value: number, method: string, what: string): nil
    if value ~= value or value == math.huge or value == -math.huge then
        error("tecs.audio." .. method .. ": " .. what .. " must be finite, got " .. tostring(value), 3)
    end
end

--- Sets a parameter by name, on the frame `at` names, ramped by the effect.
---
--- @param self the effect
--- @param name a parameter its kind has
--- @param value the target
--- @param at seconds on the output clock, or nil for at once
--- @raises string for a name the kind does not have, or a value or time that
---     is not finite
function Effect.set(exclusive self: Effect, name: string, value: number, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    local param = self.names[name]
    if param == nil then
        error("tecs.audio.Effect.set: " .. self.name .. " has no parameter named " .. name, 2)
    end
    checkFinite(value, "Effect.set", "value")
    checkAt(at, "Effect.set")
    if self.removed then
        return
    end
    self.values[param] = value
    local command = queue(mixer, audiobackend.EFFECT_PARAM, 0)
    command.data = self.id
    command.param = param
    command.gain = value
    command.at = at or 0
end

--- Reports the target last set for a parameter, or nil for one never set,
--- which is at its kind's default.
function Effect.get(borrows self: Effect, name: string): number?
    local param = self.names[name]

    return param ~= nil and self.values[param] or nil
end

--- Takes the effect out of its chain. Safe more than once.
function Effect.remove(exclusive self: Effect): nil
    if self.removed then
        return
    end
    self.removed = true
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    queue(mixer, audiobackend.EFFECT_REMOVE, 0).data = self.id
    local owner: {Effect}? = nil
    if self.onBus then
        local bus = mixer.busById[self.strip]
        owner = bus and bus.chain
    else
        local track = mixer.trackById[self.strip]
        owner = track and track.chain
    end
    if owner ~= nil then
        for index = #owner, 1, -1 do
            if owner[index] == self then
                table.remove(owner, index)
            end
        end
    end
end

--- Sets the fader, gliding there over a few milliseconds from the frame `at`
--- names.
---
--- @raises string for a gain or time that is not finite
function Bus.setGain(exclusive self: Bus, gain: number, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkFinite(gain, "Bus.setGain", "gain")
    checkAt(at, "Bus.setGain")
    self.level = gain
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.BUS_SET, self.id, audiobackend.BUS_GAIN, gain, at)
    end
end

--- Reports the fader's target.
function Bus.gain(borrows self: Bus): number
    return self.level
end

--- Mutes or unmutes everything that sums through the Bus.
---
--- @raises string for a time that is not finite
function Bus.setMuted(exclusive self: Bus, muted: boolean, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkAt(at, "Bus.setMuted")
    self.mutedFlag = muted
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.BUS_SET, self.id, audiobackend.BUS_MUTE, muted and 1 or 0, at)
    end
end

--- Reports whether the Bus is muted.
function Bus.muted(borrows self: Bus): boolean
    return self.mutedFlag
end

--- Holds every voice routed through the Bus, or through any Bus under it.
---
--- @raises string for a time that is not finite
function Bus.pause(exclusive self: Bus, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkAt(at, "Bus.pause")
    self.pausedFlag = true
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.BUS_SET, self.id, audiobackend.BUS_PAUSE, 1, at)
    end
end

--- Lets the voices under the Bus carry on.
---
--- @raises string for a time that is not finite
function Bus.resume(exclusive self: Bus, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkAt(at, "Bus.resume")
    self.pausedFlag = false
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.BUS_SET, self.id, audiobackend.BUS_PAUSE, 0, at)
    end
end

--- Reports whether this Bus is paused, not counting one above it.
function Bus.paused(borrows self: Bus): boolean
    return self.pausedFlag
end

--- Reports whether this Bus, or any Bus above it, is paused, which is what
--- holds the voices under it.
function Bus.held(borrows self: Bus): boolean
    if self.pausedFlag then
        return true
    end
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    local above = self.parentName
    local steps = 0
    while above ~= nil and steps < 64 do
        local bus = mixer.busByName[above]
        if bus == nil then
            return false
        end
        if bus.pausedFlag then
            return true
        end
        above = bus.parentName
        steps = steps + 1
    end

    return false
end

--- Reports the Bus this one sums into, nil for Master.
function Bus.parent(borrows self: Bus): string?
    return self.parentName
end

--- Routes this Bus into another, Master when nil.
---
--- @raises string for Master, which has no parent, or a parent that would
---     make a loop
function Bus.setParent(exclusive self: Bus, name: string?): nil
    if self.id == 0 then
        error("tecs.audio.Bus.setParent: Master sums into nothing", 2)
    end
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    local parent = mixer:bus(name or MASTER)
    local cursor: Bus? = parent
    while cursor ~= nil do
        if cursor == self then
            error("tecs.audio.Bus.setParent: routing " .. self.name .. " into " .. parent.name .. " makes a loop", 2)
        end
        local above = cursor.parentName
        cursor = above and mixer.busByName[above] or nil
    end
    self.parentName = parent.name
    local command = stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.BUS_SET, self.id, audiobackend.BUS_PARENT, 0, nil)
    command.data = parent.id
end

--- Returns the Bus's default Track, where a voice with this Bus as its group
--- sounds. Its polyphony is unlimited, as a group's always was.
function Bus.track(exclusive self: Bus): Track
    return assert(self.home)
end

--- Inserts an effect into the Bus's chain, at `position` from one, or at
--- the end.
---
--- @raises string when the backend cannot build it
function Bus.insert(exclusive self: Bus, spec: effectspecs.Spec, position: integer?): Effect
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    return insertEffect(mixer, self.chain, true, self.id, spec, position)
end

--- Returns the Bus's chain in order, a fresh table.
function Bus.effects(borrows self: Bus): {Effect}
    local out: {Effect} = {}
    for index = 1, #self.chain do
        out[index] = self.chain[index]
    end

    return out
end

--- Removes the Bus. What routed into it routes into its parent, and its
--- default Track's voices move to Master's.
---
--- @raises string for Master
function Bus.destroy(exclusive self: Bus): nil
    if self.id == 0 then
        error("tecs.audio.Bus.destroy: Master cannot be destroyed", 2)
    end
    if self.destroyed then
        return
    end
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    local parent = self.parentName or MASTER
    self.destroyed = true
    for _, track in pairs(mixer.trackById) do
        if track.busName == self.name then
            track.busName = parent
        end
        track.sendLevels[self.name] = nil
    end
    for _, bus in pairs(mixer.busByName) do
        if bus.parentName == self.name then
            bus.parentName = parent
        end
    end
    local home = self.home
    if home ~= nil then
        home.destroyed = true
        mixer.trackById[home.id] = nil
        for slot = 1, #mixer.voices do
            local voice = mixer.voices[slot]
            if voice.sounding and voice.track == home.id then
                voice.track = 0
                voice.group = nil
            end
        end
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_DESTROY, home.id, 0, 0, nil)
    end
    stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.BUS_DESTROY, self.id, 0, 0, nil)
    mixer.busByName[self.name] = nil
    mixer.busById[self.id] = nil
    mixer.groupGains[self.name] = nil
    mixer.groupMutedFlags[self.name] = nil
    mixer.groupPausedFlags[self.name] = nil
end

--- Plays a clip on this Track. `options.track` is this Track whatever it
--- says.
function Track.play(exclusive self: Track, sound: Playable?, options: PlayOptions?): integer
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    if self.destroyed then
        return refuse(mixer, 0, options or {}, "track")
    end
    local given = options or {}
    local copy: PlayOptions = {}
    for key, value in pairs(given as {[string]: any}) do
        (copy as {[string]: any})[key] = value
    end
    copy.track = mixer.trackById[self.id]

    return mixer:play(sound, copy)
end

--- Stops every voice on this Track.
function Track.stopAll(exclusive self: Track, fadeOut: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    for slot = 1, #mixer.voices do
        local voice = mixer.voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding and voice.track == self.id then
            mixer:stop(handleOf(mixer, slot), fadeOut)
        end
    end
end

--- Counts the voices sounding on this Track, a fading one not included.
function Track.sounding(borrows self: Track): integer
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    return trackSounding(mixer, self.id)
end

--- Sets the fader, gliding there from the frame `at` names.
---
--- @raises string for a gain or time that is not finite
function Track.setGain(exclusive self: Track, gain: number, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkFinite(gain, "Track.setGain", "gain")
    checkAt(at, "Track.setGain")
    self.level = gain
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_SET, self.id, audiobackend.TRACK_GAIN, gain, at)
    end
end

--- Reports the fader's target.
function Track.gain(borrows self: Track): number
    return self.level
end

--- Mutes or unmutes the Track.
---
--- @raises string for a time that is not finite
function Track.setMuted(exclusive self: Track, muted: boolean, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkAt(at, "Track.setMuted")
    self.mutedFlag = muted
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_SET, self.id, audiobackend.TRACK_MUTE, muted and 1 or 0, at)
    end
end

--- Reports whether the Track is muted.
function Track.muted(borrows self: Track): boolean
    return self.mutedFlag
end

--- Solos the Track: while any Track is soloed, every Track that is not is
--- silent. Return buses are not Tracks, so a soloed Track's sends still ring.
---
--- @raises string for a time that is not finite
function Track.setSolo(exclusive self: Track, solo: boolean, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkAt(at, "Track.setSolo")
    self.soloFlag = solo
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_SET, self.id, audiobackend.TRACK_SOLO, solo and 1 or 0, at)
    end
end

--- Reports whether the Track is soloed.
function Track.solo(borrows self: Track): boolean
    return self.soloFlag
end

--- Reports the Bus the Track routes to.
function Track.bus(borrows self: Track): string
    return self.busName
end

--- Routes the Track to another Bus, made if it does not exist.
---
--- @raises string for a Bus's default Track, which stays on its Bus
function Track.setBus(exclusive self: Track, name: string): nil
    if self.isDefault then
        error("tecs.audio.Track.setBus: a Bus's default Track stays on its Bus", 2)
    end
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    local bus = mixer:bus(name)
    self.busName = bus.name
    local command = stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_SET, self.id, audiobackend.TRACK_BUS, 0, nil)
    command.data = bus.id
    for slot = 1, #mixer.voices do
        local voice = mixer.voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding and voice.track == self.id then
            voice.group = bus.name ~= MASTER and bus.name or nil
        end
    end
end

--- Places the Track's sum against the listener, folded to mono, on the frame
--- `at` names. The frame is the listener's: x right, y up, z behind.
---
--- @raises string for a position or time that is not finite
function Track.setPosition(exclusive self: Track, x: number, y: number, z: number, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkFinite(x, "Track.setPosition", "x")
    checkFinite(y, "Track.setPosition", "y")
    checkFinite(z, "Track.setPosition", "z")
    checkAt(at, "Track.setPosition")
    self.placed = "spatial"
    self.px = x
    self.py = y
    self.pz = z
    if not self.destroyed then
        local command = stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_SET, self.id, audiobackend.TRACK_POSITION, 0, at)
        command.x = x
        command.y = y
        command.z = z
    end
end

--- Scales the Track's left and right channels.
---
--- @raises string for a gain or time that is not finite
function Track.setStereo(exclusive self: Track, left: number, right: number, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkFinite(left, "Track.setStereo", "left")
    checkFinite(right, "Track.setStereo", "right")
    checkAt(at, "Track.setStereo")
    self.placed = "stereo"
    self.px = left
    self.py = right
    if not self.destroyed then
        local command = stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_SET, self.id, audiobackend.TRACK_STEREO, 0, at)
        command.x = left
        command.y = right
    end
end

--- Returns the Track to unplaced mixing.
---
--- @raises string for a time that is not finite
function Track.clearPlacement(exclusive self: Track, at: number?): nil
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    checkAt(at, "Track.clearPlacement")
    self.placed = "none"
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_SET, self.id, audiobackend.TRACK_UNPLACED, 0, at)
    end
end

--- Reports the Track's position when it is spatial.
function Track.position(borrows self: Track): (number?, number?, number?)
    if self.placed == "spatial" then
        return self.px, self.py, self.pz
    else
        return nil, nil, nil
    end
end

--- Sends the Track, after its fader, to a return Bus at `level`, made if it
--- does not exist. Zero removes the send.
---
--- @raises string for Master, which every Track already reaches, or a level
---     or time that is not finite
function Track.send(exclusive self: Track, bus: string, level: number, at: number?): nil
    checkFinite(level, "Track.send", "level")
    checkAt(at, "Track.send")
    if bus == MASTER then
        error("tecs.audio.Track.send: a send goes to a return Bus, not Master", 2)
    end
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    local target = mixer:bus(bus)
    if level > 0 then
        self.sendLevels[bus] = level
    else
        self.sendLevels[bus] = nil
    end
    if not self.destroyed then
        stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.SEND, self.id, target.id, level, at)
    end
end

--- Returns the Track's sends by Bus name, a fresh table.
function Track.sends(borrows self: Track): {[string]: number}
    local out: {[string]: number} = {}
    for name, level in pairs(self.sendLevels) do
        out[name] = level
    end

    return out
end

--- Reports how many voices the Track holds before it steals, zero for no
--- limit.
function Track.polyphony(borrows self: Track): integer
    return self.limit
end

--- Sets how many voices the Track holds before it steals. Lowering it stops
--- none of those already sounding.
---
--- @raises string for a negative count
function Track.setPolyphony(exclusive self: Track, voices: integer): nil
    if voices < 0 then
        error("tecs.audio.Track.setPolyphony: voices must not be negative", 2)
    end
    self.limit = voices
end

--- Inserts an effect into the Track's chain, at `position` from one, or at
--- the end.
---
--- @raises string when the backend cannot build it
function Track.insert(exclusive self: Track, spec: effectspecs.Spec, position: integer?): Effect
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    return insertEffect(mixer, self.chain, false, self.id, spec, position)
end

--- Returns the Track's chain in order, a fresh table.
function Track.effects(borrows self: Track): {Effect}
    local out: {Effect} = {}
    for index = 1, #self.chain do
        out[index] = self.chain[index]
    end

    return out
end

--- Removes the Track, stopping every voice on it. Safe more than once.
---
--- @raises string for a Bus's default Track, which goes with its Bus
function Track.destroy(exclusive self: Track): nil
    if self.isDefault then
        error("tecs.audio.Track.destroy: a Bus's default Track goes with its Bus", 2)
    end
    if self.destroyed then
        return
    end
    self:stopAll()
    self.destroyed = true
    local mixer = mixerOf(self.owner)
    mixer.trackById[self.id] = nil
    stripCommand(mixer, audiobackend.TRACK_DESTROY, self.id, 0, 0, nil)
end

--- Creates the mixer a game plays sound through.
---
--- Never raises for want of hardware. A machine with no sound card gets an
--- object whose calls all succeed and produce nothing, because few games
--- require an audio device while many test machines lack one.
---
--- @param config the settings, or nil for the defaults
--- @return the mixer, which the caller has to `destroy`
--- @raises string when `maxVoices` falls outside one to 65535
export function newAudio(config: Config?): Audio
    local maxVoices = (config and config.maxVoices) or DEFAULT_MAX_VOICES
    if maxVoices < 1 or maxVoices >= SLOT_SPAN then
        error("tecs.audio.newAudio: maxVoices must be between 1 and " .. tostring(SLOT_SPAN - 1), 2)
    end
    local frequency = (config and config.frequency) or DEFAULT_FREQUENCY
    local channels = (config and config.channels) or DEFAULT_CHANNELS

    local output = (config and config.output) or "device"
    if output ~= "device" and output ~= "void" and output ~= "memory" then
        error("tecs.audio.newAudio: output must be \"device\", \"void\" or \"memory\"", 2)
    end
    local backend = config and config.backend
    if backend == nil then
        local loaded, native = pcall(require, "tecs.platform.audionative")
        if loaded then
            local opener = OPENERS[output] or "openOrVoid"
            local opened, failure = (native as {[string]: any})[opener](frequency, channels, maxVoices + STEAL_HEADROOM)
            backend = opened as audiobackend.Backend?
            if backend == nil then
                LOGGER:info("no audio output: %s", tostring(failure))
            end
        else
            LOGGER:info("no audio backend: %s", tostring(native))
        end
    end
    if backend == nil then
        backend = audiobackend.silent(frequency)
    end

    local voices: {Voice} = {}
    local free: {integer} = {}
    for slot = 1, maxVoices do
        voices[
            slot
        ] = new Voice(
            clip = 0,
            gain = 1,
            applied = -1,
            pitch = 1,
            group = nil,
            track = 0,
            priority = 0,
            strip = nil,
            order = 0,
            key = nil,
            generation = 0,
            sounding = false,
            stopping = false,
            paused = false,
            owned = false,
            referenced = false,
            loop = false,
            nearest = false,
            spatial = false,
            x = 0,
            y = 0,
            z = 0,
            stereo = false,
            left = 1,
            right = 1
        )
        -- Taken from the end, so the first sound of a run uses slot one and a
        -- log reads in the order things happened.
        free[slot] = maxVoices + 1 - slot
    end

    local finished: {integer} = {}
    for slot = 1, maxVoices do
        finished[slot] = 0
    end

    local self = new Audio(
        closed = false,
        available = backend.available,
        backend = backend,
        streamSeconds = (config and config.streamSeconds) or DEFAULT_STREAM_SECONDS,
        voices = voices,
        free = free,
        live = 0,
        master = 1,
        mutedFlag = false,
        groupGains = {},
        groupMutedFlags = {},
        groupPausedFlags = {},
        limits = {},
        buckets = {},
        clipsById = {},
        variance = new random.Xoshiro128(),
        elapsed = 0,
        pending = {},
        pendingCount = 0,
        finished = finished,
        playSystem = nil,
        outputChanges = 0,
        outputDrops = 0,
        outputReopens = 0,
        history = {},
        sequence = 0,
        refusals = 0,
        stolen = 0,
        stepRemainder = 0,
        -- A memory output is always held; anything else starts running.
        held = backend.hold ~= nil and backend.hold(false),
        touched = false,
        busByName = {},
        busById = {},
        trackById = {},
        nextBus = 1,
        nextTrack = 1,
        nextEffect = 1,
        nextOrder = 0,
        defaultPolyphony = (config and config.polyphony) or DEFAULT_POLYPHONY,
        clockline = nil,
        registry = 0,
        soundClips = setmetatable({}, {__mode = "k"}),
        zones = {},
        timelineList = {}
    )
    nextMixer = nextMixer + 1
    self.registry = nextMixer
    mixers[nextMixer] = self
    -- Master and its default Track exist on the backend from the start; these
    -- are their handles here.
    local master = newBus(self, MASTER, 0, nil)
    self.busByName[MASTER] = master
    self.busById[0] = master
    local home = newTrack(self, 0, MASTER, 0)
    home.isDefault = true
    master.home = home
    self.trackById[0] = home
    if not (config and config.limiter == false) then
        master:insert(effectspecs.limiter())
    end
    if not self.available then
        LOGGER:info("no audio output; sound is silent for this run")
    end

    return self
end

--- Sends every queued command to the backend.
---
--- `update` and the audio pass both call this, so a game following the ordinary
--- frame never needs it. Call it directly only to close the gap between a
--- command and the buffer it reaches.
---
--- @param self the mixer whose queue is flushed
function Audio.flush(exclusive self: Audio): nil
    if self.pendingCount == 0 then
        return
    end
    self.backend.submit(self.pending, self.pendingCount)
    self.pendingCount = 0
end

--- Reports the output clock every `at` on this surface is on.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return seconds: the frames the backend has mixed over `frequency`. It
---     counts what the device has been handed rather than what has been heard,
---     never runs backwards, keeps running through a device reopen and on a
---     void output, and reads zero on a closed mixer and on a backend without a
---     clock
function Audio.now(borrows self: Audio): number
    local clock = self.backend.now
    if self.closed or clock == nil then
        return 0
    end

    return clock()
end

--- Reports the output's frames per second, which turns a count of output
--- frames into an `at`.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return frames per second. The silent backend reports the frequency the
---     mixer was configured for, so a count of frames over it is still a time.
function Audio.frequency(borrows self: Audio): integer
    return self.backend.frequency
end

--- Stops everything and closes the output.
---
--- Stops every voice without a fade, moves every clip to `"released"`, and
--- removes this mixer from every world that installed it. There is no
--- reopening: make a new instance instead.
---
--- @param self the mixer to close
function Audio.destroy(exclusive self: Audio): nil
    if self.closed then
        return
    end
    self.closed = true
    for slot = 1, #self.voices do
        if self.voices[slot].sounding then
            queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handleOf(self, slot))
            release(self, slot)
        end
    end
    self:flush()
    self.buckets = {}

    for id, clip in pairs(self.clipsById) do
        if clip.status == "ready" then
            self.backend.releaseClip(id)
        end
        clip.resident = false
        clip.status = "released"
    end
    self.clipsById = {}

    self.backend.close()
    self.available = false

    -- Every world this was installed into stops naming it, because nothing
    -- here can open an output again and a world still answering with this
    -- mixer would hand the debug tools one that can only report itself
    -- unavailable.
    for world, mixer in pairs(installed) do
        if mixer == self then
            installed[world] = nil
        end
    end
end

--- Returns the decoder names this build linked.
---
--- What a build asked for and what it got are different questions, and this
--- answers the second.
---
--- @param self the mixer to ask
--- @return the names in the backend's own order rather than sorted, and empty
---     when no output opened. A fresh table each call.
function Audio.decoders(borrows self: Audio): {string}
    return self.backend.decoders()
end

--- Reports what became of the output device.
---
--- `available` and `failureReason` describe the mixer, and a device that drops
--- moves neither: the backend reopens the default output at whatever format it
--- has now, the mix keeps running at its own, and every voice carries on. This
--- is where such a drop shows, and `update` logs each drop and each reopen
--- under `tecs.audio`.
---
--- @param self the mixer to ask
--- @return the backend's report. A backend with nothing to report on answers
---     `"playing"` at the mix's own format while `available`, `"failed"` after
---     `failureReason`, and `"offline"` otherwise. A fresh record each call.
function Audio.output(borrows self: Audio): audiobackend.OutputStatus
    local report = self.backend.output
    if report ~= nil and not self.closed then
        return report()
    end
    local state: "offline" | "playing" | "reopening" | "failed" = "offline"
    local frequency: integer = 0
    local channels: integer = 0
    if self.failureReason ~= nil then
        state = "failed"
    elseif self.available then
        state = "playing"
        frequency = self.backend.frequency
        channels = self.backend.channels
    end

    return new audiobackend.OutputStatus(
        state = state,
        drops = 0,
        reopens = 0,
        reason = nil,
        frequency = frequency,
        channels = channels
    )
end

--- Loads a sound and returns its cached clip.
---
--- Loading the same path twice returns the same clip: a clip is the file, and
--- playing it twice is two voices reading one clip. `options.stream` overrides
--- the duration threshold that otherwise decides whether it stays in memory.
---
--- @param self the mixer that holds the clip
--- @param path the non-empty file path, which is also the clip's identity
--- @param options the residency override, read only by the first call for a path
--- @return the cached clip, or nil when the file could not be read
--- @return the reason, when the first return is nil
--- @raises string when the path is empty
function Audio.load(exclusive self: Audio, path: string, options: LoadOptions?): (Clip?, string?)
    local id = clipId(path)
    local existing = self.clipsById[id]
    if existing ~= nil then
        if existing.status == "ready" then
            return existing
        end

        return nil, existing.error or ("cannot load " .. path)
    end

    local mode: audiobackend.LoadMode = "auto"
    local stream = options and options.stream
    if stream == true then
        mode = "stream"
    elseif stream == false then
        mode = "resident"
    end

    local info, failure = self.backend.loadClip(path, id, mode, self.streamSeconds)
    if info == nil then
        local reason = failure or ("cannot load " .. path)
        self.clipsById[
            id
        ] = new Clip(
            path = path,
            id = id,
            status = "failed",
            error = reason,
            duration = 0,
            resident = false,
            memory = false
        )
        LOGGER:error("cannot load '%s': %s", path, reason)

        return nil, reason
    end

    local clip = new Clip(
        path = path,
        id = id,
        status = "ready",
        error = nil,
        duration = info.duration,
        resident = info.resident,
        memory = false
    )
    self.clipsById[id] = clip

    return clip
end

--- Decodes a sound from the bytes of a whole encoded file and returns its
--- cached clip.
---
--- The bytes are what the file would hold on disk, container and all, in any
--- format `load` reads. A caller holding raw samples builds the container
--- itself. The clip is always resident and keeps decoded samples rather than
--- the bytes, which the caller may drop once this returns.
---
--- `name` is the clip's identity on the same terms as a path is for `load`,
--- and shares its namespace: loading a name already loaded, from bytes or from
--- a file, returns that clip without reading `bytes`, and a failure is cached
--- the same way. A [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) row and a snapshot carry the
--- name, so a restored row starts once this mixer has loaded it again.
---
--- @param self the mixer that holds the clip
--- @param name the non-empty identity, which is not a path and touches no file.
---     Its extension, when it has one, may hint the format to the decoder,
---     which reads the content either way.
--- @param bytes the whole encoded file, which may contain zero bytes
--- @param options the residency override, where only an unset or false
---     `stream` is accepted
--- @return the cached clip, or nil when the bytes could not be decoded
--- @return the reason, when the first return is nil
--- @raises string when the name is empty
--- @raises string when `options.stream` is true
function Audio.loadBytes(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, bytes: string, options: LoadOptions?): (Clip?, string?)
    if name == "" then
        error("tecs.audio.loadBytes: a clip name cannot be empty", 2)
    end
    if options ~= nil and options.stream == true then
        error("tecs.audio.loadBytes: a clip from memory cannot stream, because there is no file to read from", 2)
    end
    local id = clipId(name)
    local existing = self.clipsById[id]
    if existing ~= nil then
        if existing.status == "ready" then
            return existing
        end

        return nil, existing.error or ("cannot decode " .. name)
    end

    local info: audiobackend.ClipInfo? = nil
    local failure: string? = nil
    local decode = self.backend.loadClipBytes
    if decode == nil then
        failure = "this audio backend cannot decode a clip from memory"
    else
        info, failure = decode(name, bytes, id)
    end
    if info == nil then
        local reason = failure or ("cannot decode " .. name)
        self.clipsById[
            id
        ] = new Clip(
            path = name,
            id = id,
            status = "failed",
            error = reason,
            duration = 0,
            resident = false,
            memory = true
        )
        LOGGER:error("cannot decode '%s': %s", name, reason)

        return nil, reason
    end

    local clip = new Clip(
        path = name,
        id = id,
        status = "ready",
        error = nil,
        duration = info.duration,
        resident = info.resident,
        memory = true
    )
    self.clipsById[id] = clip

    return clip
end

--- Returns the clip an index names.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param id an index from `clipId` or from a `Sound.clip` field
--- @return the clip, or nil when this mixer has not loaded it. Every mixer
---     shares clip indices but not clips.
function Audio.clip(borrows self: Audio, id: integer): Clip?
    return self.clipsById[id]
end

--- Returns every loaded clip in index order.
---
--- For introspection: it builds a list per call, so nothing on a frame's path
--- should read it.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return a fresh list each call, holding the live clip records rather than
---     copies, so their `status` moves under a caller that keeps one
function Audio.clips(borrows self: Audio): {Clip}
    local ids: {integer} = {}
    for id in pairs(self.clipsById) do
        ids[#ids + 1] = id
    end
    table.sort(ids)
    local list: {Clip} = {}
    for index = 1, #ids do
        list[index] = assert(self.clipsById[ids[index]])
    end

    return list
end

--- Re-reads a clip's file over the clip already loaded from it.
---
--- A clip's index is its path's, so an edited file comes back under the index
--- every [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) row already carries and nothing in the
--- world is touched. A streamed clip holds nothing to replace, so this reports
--- success and the next voice to start reads what is on disk now. A clip
--- `loadBytes` decoded has no file and kept no bytes, so this reports false
--- for it and leaves it as it was.
---
--- Blocking, like every other reload: it is a debug operation, and answering
--- before the file has been read would report a success that had not happened.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the clip
--- @param path the path originally used to load the clip
--- @return whether the clip was re-read
--- @return the reason, when it was not
function Audio.reload(exclusive self: Audio, path: string): (boolean, string?)
    local id = clipIds[path]
    local clip = id ~= nil and self.clipsById[id] or nil
    if clip == nil then
        return false, "nothing has loaded '" .. path .. "', so there is no clip to re-read"
    end
    if clip.memory then
        return false, "'" .. path .. "' was decoded from bytes in memory, so there is no file to re-read"
    end
    if not clip.resident then
        return true
    end

    local info, failure = self.backend.loadClip(path, clip.id, "resident", self.streamSeconds)
    if info == nil then
        return false, "'" .. path .. "' did not load: " .. (failure or "no reason given")
    end
    clip.duration = info.duration
    clip.resident = info.resident
    clip.status = "ready"
    clip.error = nil

    return true
end

--- Plays a clip and returns a handle, or zero when nothing started.
---
--- Zero means the clip is not loaded, loading failed, a key's limit or cooldown
--- declined it, or every voice is busy. None of those is worth raising over: a
--- sound that does not play is not a reason for a frame to stop.
---
--- A key's limit drops the new voice and never steals an older one, which is
--- the same reasoning a full voice pool follows.
---
--- @param self the mixer that starts the voice
--- @param sound a clip this mixer loaded, a `PcmSound` asset, or the handle
---     one was requested under, whose current Sound plays. Nil, a clip still
---     loading, a failed one and a Sound that does not decode start nothing.
--- @param options read once, with no later change followed
--- @return the handle the voice methods take, or zero when nothing started. It
---     stays valid while the voice sounds, goes inert for good once it does
---     not, is never reissued, and requires no release.
function Audio.play(exclusive self: Audio, sound: Playable?, options: PlayOptions?): integer
    checkAt(options and options.at, "play")
    return start(self, clipOf(self, sound), options or {}, false)
end

--- Stops a voice. A handle to one that has already ended does nothing.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a stale one is a no-op, as is one already fading out: a second
---     stop does not shorten a fade in progress
--- @param fadeOut seconds to ramp down over, a duration rather than a moment to
---     finish at. Omitted or zero frees the slot within this call, unless `at`
---     is given. The ramp is the backend's, so it follows the audio clock
---     rather than the frame rate.
--- @param at seconds on the output clock `now` reads to stop on, on exactly
---     that output frame, where the fade starts. Omitted, zero and a time
---     already mixed mean at once. A timed stop keeps the slot until the
---     backend reports the voice finished, as a fade does, and the voice takes
---     no further instruction meanwhile.
function Audio.stop(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, fadeOut: number?, at: number?): nil
    checkAt(at, "stop")
    local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
    if voice == nil or voice.stopping then
        return
    end
    local seconds = fadeOut or 0
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handle)
    command.fade = seconds > 0 and seconds or 0
    command.at = at or 0
    record(self, "stop", handle, at, voice.clip, voice.group, nil)
    if seconds <= 0 and not isTimed(at) then
        release(self, slotOf(handle))

        return
    end
    voice.stopping = true
    -- A paused voice does not advance, so its fade would never finish and the
    -- slot would never come back.
    voice.paused = false
end

--- Stops every voice, over `fadeOut` seconds when that is given.
---
--- Reaches every voice, whichever group it is in and whether a
--- [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) component started it or `play` did. A row still
--- asking to sound starts a fresh voice on the next audio pass, so this
--- silences a world rather than keeping it silent.
---
--- @param self the mixer to silence
--- @param fadeOut seconds, a duration. Omitted or zero frees every slot within
---     this call, and a positive one unpauses the voices it fades.
function Audio.stopAll(exclusive self: Audio, fadeOut: number?): nil
    local seconds = fadeOut or 0
    for slot = 1, #self.voices do
        local voice = self.voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding then
            record(self, "stop", handleOf(self, slot), nil, voice.clip, voice.group, nil)
            queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handleOf(self, slot)).fade = seconds > 0 and seconds or 0
            if seconds > 0 then
                voice.stopping = true
                voice.paused = false
            else
                release(self, slot)
            end
        end
    end
end

--- Reports whether a handle still names a sounding voice.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param handle the handle to test
--- @return whether the voice has not finished. A paused or fading voice counts.
---     A voice that ended on its own reads true until the next `update` reaps
---     it, since nothing here is told the instant it finished.
function Audio.playing(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): boolean
    return voiceOf(self, handle) ~= nil
end

--- Reports whether a handle names a paused voice.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param handle the handle to test
--- @return whether a hold applies, whether it came from `pause` on this handle
---     or from `pauseGroup` on its group. The two are not told apart.
function Audio.paused(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): boolean
    local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
    if voice == nil then
        return false
    end
    if voice.paused then
        return true
    end
    local track = self.trackById[voice.track]
    local bus = track and self.busByName[track.busName]

    return bus ~= nil and bus:held()
end

--- Holds a voice where it is, keeping its slot until something resumes or
--- stops it.
---
--- Neither this nor `resume` takes a fade, and that is a decision rather than an
--- omission. A faded pause would be a ramp run from here, a command that takes effect
--- later and needs its own answer for what a stop or a group gain during the ramp
--- means. A game wanting that builds it from `tell`, `stop` with a fade, and a later
--- `play` with `start` and `fadeIn`.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for a handle naming nothing, one already paused, and
---     one fading out. Not holder counted: one `resume` undoes any number of
---     these.
function Audio.pause(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): nil
    local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
    if voice == nil or voice.paused or voice.stopping then
        return
    end
    voice.paused = true
    queue(self, audiobackend.PAUSE, handle)
end

--- Lets a paused voice carry on.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for a handle naming nothing and for one not paused.
---     Resumes a voice its group paused, and the group's hold does not put it
---     back.
function Audio.resume(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): nil
    local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
    if voice == nil or not voice.paused then
        return
    end
    voice.paused = false
    queue(self, audiobackend.RESUME, handle)
end

--- Sets a voice's gain, before its group's and the master's.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for one naming nothing
--- @param gain linear amplitude rather than decibels. It takes effect on the
---     next buffer the backend fills, or on the frame `at` names, rather than
---     ramping, so a large jump on a sounding voice can be audible as a step.
--- @param at seconds on the output clock `now` reads, on exactly that output
---     frame. Omitted, zero and a time already mixed mean at once. A timed
---     value is always sent; an untimed one equal to the last value sent is
---     skipped, whenever the earlier one was due.
function Audio.setGain(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, gain: number, at: number?): nil
    checkAt(at, "setGain")
    local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
    if voice == nil then
        return
    end
    voice.gain = gain
    applyGain(self, slotOf(handle), at)
end

--- Sets a voice's playback rate.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for one naming nothing
--- @param ratio a resampling rate rather than an interval, so it changes how
---     long the clip takes and a looped voice's period moves with it
--- @param at seconds on the output clock `now` reads, on the terms of
---     `setGain`. The output frame it names is the first read at the new rate:
---     it sits where the old rate brought the voice, and the frame after it is
---     one new step further on.
function Audio.setPitch(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, ratio: number, at: number?): nil
    checkAt(at, "setPitch")
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return
    end
    applyPitch(self, slotOf(handle), ratio, at)
end

--- Changes how a voice reads between the frames of its clip, part way through.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for one naming nothing
--- @param mode see [`Resample`](tecs.audio.Resample)
--- @param at seconds on the output clock `now` reads, on the terms of `setGain`
function Audio.setResample(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, mode: Resample, at: number?): nil
    checkAt(at, "setResample")
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return
    end
    applyResample(self, slotOf(handle), mode == "nearest", at)
end

--- Reports how a voice reads between the frames of its clip.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param handle the handle to test
--- @return what this module last sent rather than a reading from the backend,
---     and nil for a handle naming nothing
function Audio.resample(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): Resample?
    local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
    if voice == nil then
        return nil
    end

    return voice.nearest and "nearest" or "linear"
end

--- Changes whether a voice repeats, part way through.
---
--- Clearing this on a looping piece of music lets it play out to its end rather
--- than cutting it, and setting it on a one-shot keeps it going. It reaches a
--- sounding voice only: a stopped one takes its answer from the next play.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for one naming nothing
--- @param loop true repeats forever, and there is no finite repeat count here.
---     Setting it does not move the loop point, which stays where `play` put it.
--- @param at seconds on the output clock `now` reads, on the terms of `setGain`
function Audio.setLoop(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, loop: boolean, at: number?): nil
    checkAt(at, "setLoop")
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return
    end
    applyLoop(self, slotOf(handle), loop == true, at)
end

--- Reports whether a voice repeats at the end.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param handle the handle to test
--- @return what this module last told the backend rather than a reading from
---     it, and false for a handle naming nothing
function Audio.looping(borrows self: Audio, handle: integer): boolean
    local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)

    return voice ~= nil and voice.loop
end

--- Moves a voice's read position.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle the voice to move
--- @param seconds from the start of the clip rather than from where the voice
---     is now, and not from the loop point
--- @return false when the handle names nothing or the input refused the seek.
---     A true does not promise the exact sample: some decoders reach only the
---     nearest point they can.
function Audio.seek(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, seconds: number): boolean
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return false
    end
    self:flush()

    return self.backend.seek(handle, seconds)
end

--- Returns a voice's read position in seconds.
---
--- A paused voice reports where it stopped, which with `seek` and a later
--- `play` carrying `start` and `fadeIn` is what taking a sound back where it
--- left off is built from.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle the voice to read
--- @return seconds from the start of the clip, so a looped voice's reading
---     falls back at each repeat rather than accumulating. Nil when the handle
---     names nothing and nil when the backend will not say, which are not told
---     apart here.
function Audio.tell(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): number?
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return nil
    end
    self:flush()

    return self.backend.position(handle)
end

--- Places a voice in space.
---
--- Deprecated: place a Track instead, with `Track.setPosition`, or a `Sound`
--- row with a `Listener`, which places its emitter Track. A voice's own
--- placement stays for code written before Tracks.
---
--- Replaces a pan set by `setStereo` rather than combining with it. Positioning
--- folds the input down to mono before placing it, so a stereo clip loses its
--- stereo image when positioned.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for one naming nothing
--- @param x positive to the right of the listener, which sits at the origin
--- @param y positive above the listener. World Y runs down, so world
---     coordinates need their Y negated on the way in.
--- @param z positive behind the listener
--- @param at seconds on the output clock `now` reads, on the terms of `setGain`
function Audio.setPosition(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, x: number, y: number, z: number, at: number?): nil
    checkAt(at, "setPosition")
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return
    end
    applyPosition(self, slotOf(handle), x, y, z, at)
end

--- Pins a voice to the front pair of speakers at explicit gains.
---
--- Deprecated: pan a Track instead, with `Track.setStereo`. A voice's own
--- placement stays for code written before Tracks.
---
--- A pan rather than a position, and usually what a game laid out on a plane
--- wants: there is no listener to subtract and no distance model to argue with,
--- only how much of this comes out of each side. Replaces a position set by
--- `setPosition` rather than combining with it.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for one naming nothing
--- @param left gain out of the left speaker on the same linear scale as
---     `setGain`, multiplying with the voice, group and master gains rather
---     than replacing any of them
--- @param right gain out of the right speaker. There is no normalization
---     between the two: one and one is the sound at full out of both sides.
--- @param at seconds on the output clock `now` reads, on the terms of `setGain`
function Audio.setStereo(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, left: number, right: number, at: number?): nil
    checkAt(at, "setStereo")
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return
    end
    applyStereo(self, slotOf(handle), left, right, at)
end

--- Returns a voice to unpositioned mixing, out of either placement.
---
--- Deprecated: return a Track to unplaced mixing with
--- `Track.clearPlacement` instead.
---
--- One call answers for both, because a voice holds one placement.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the voice
--- @param handle a no-op for one naming nothing and for a voice never placed
function Audio.clearSpatial(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer): nil
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return
    end
    applyUnplaced(self, slotOf(handle))
end

--- Scales everything.
---
--- One number on the output, so this costs the same whether one voice is
--- sounding or every voice is. Setting it while muted changes the level a later
--- unmute returns to and nothing audible now, which is what a volume slider
--- moved with the sound off should do.
---
--- @param self the mixer to scale
--- @param gain linear amplitude rather than decibels
function Audio.setMasterGain(exclusive self: Audio, gain: number): nil
    self.master = gain
    if not self.mutedFlag then
        queue(self, audiobackend.SET_MASTER_GAIN, 0).gain = gain
    end
end

--- Returns master gain, whether or not mute holds it down.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return the level rather than what is audible
function Audio.masterGain(borrows self: Audio): number
    return self.master
end

--- Silences everything without discarding the master gain.
---
--- It does not fan out to the groups: `groupMuted` answers whether a group is
--- silenced, and writing every group's bit here would overwrite the answers an
--- unmute has to put back.
---
--- @param self the mixer to mute
--- @param muted whether the output is held at zero
function Audio.setMuted(exclusive self: Audio, muted: boolean): nil
    local wanted = muted == true
    if self.mutedFlag == wanted then
        return
    end
    self.mutedFlag = wanted
    queue(self, audiobackend.SET_MASTER_GAIN, 0).gain = wanted and 0 or self.master
end

--- Reports whether master mute holds the output down.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return whether mute applies
function Audio.muted(borrows self: Audio): boolean
    return self.mutedFlag
end

--- Returns the number of voices sounding now, paused and fading ones included.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return slots in use, so it drops when `update` reaps a voice rather than
---     the instant one ends
function Audio.sounding(borrows self: Audio): integer
    return self.live
end

--- Returns the configured ceiling on simultaneous voices.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return what `newAudio` was given, fixed for the instance's life
function Audio.maxVoices(borrows self: Audio): integer
    return #self.voices
end

--- Scales every voice in a group, and every voice that joins it later.
---
--- Deprecated: a group is a Bus; prefer `mixer:bus(name):setGain`.
---
--- Composed here rather than sent as one number for the group, because a
--- per-group gain on the backend would overwrite what each voice asked for.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the group
--- @param name needs no declaring, so setting a gain on a name nothing is in is
---     how a game configures a group before anything joins it
--- @param gain linear amplitude multiplied with each voice's own. There is no
---     removing it: set one to return the group to unscaled.
function Audio.setGroupGain(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, gain: number): nil
    self.groupGains[name] = gain
    local bus = self:bus(name)
    bus:setGain(gain)
end

--- Returns a group's gain.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param name the group name
--- @return the level rather than what is audible, and one for a group nothing
---     has set
function Audio.groupGain(borrows self: Audio, name: string): number
    return self.groupGains[name] or 1
end

--- Silences a group without discarding the level it was set to.
---
--- Deprecated: a group is a Bus; prefer `mixer:bus(name):setMuted`.
---
--- Every voice in the group contributes zero while this holds, and an unmute
--- puts each one back at its own gain times `groupGain`.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the group
--- @param name the group name
--- @param muted whether the group contributes zero
function Audio.setGroupMuted(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, muted: boolean): nil
    local wanted = muted == true
    if (self.groupMutedFlags[name] == true) == wanted then
        return
    end
    if wanted then
        self.groupMutedFlags[name] = true
    else
        self.groupMutedFlags[name] = nil
    end
    local bus = self:bus(name)
    bus:setMuted(wanted)
end

--- Reports whether mute applies to a group.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param name the group name
--- @return whether the group is silenced
function Audio.groupMuted(borrows self: Audio, name: string): boolean
    return self.groupMutedFlags[name] == true
end

--- Holds every voice in a group, and every voice that joins it later.
---
--- Deprecated: a group is a Bus; prefer `mixer:bus(name):pause`.
---
--- The mixer records the pause as well as sending it, because a voice started
--- into a paused group would otherwise be the one thing still heard.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the group
--- @param name not holder counted: one `resumeGroup` undoes any number of
---     these. The group is a Bus, and its pause holds every voice routed
---     through it, so resuming one voice does not take it out of the hold.
function Audio.pauseGroup(exclusive self: Audio, name: string): nil
    self.groupPausedFlags[name] = true
    local bus = self:bus(name)
    bus:pause()
end

--- Lets a paused group carry on, and lets later joiners start sounding.
---
--- Deprecated: a group is a Bus; prefer `mixer:bus(name):resume`.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the group
--- @param name the group name
function Audio.resumeGroup(exclusive self: Audio, name: string): nil
    self.groupPausedFlags[name] = nil
    local bus = self:bus(name)
    bus:resume()
end

--- Reports whether a group holds its current and future voices.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param name the group name
--- @return whether the group is paused
function Audio.groupPaused(borrows self: Audio, name: string): boolean
    return self.groupPausedFlags[name] == true
end

--- Appends a name unless it is already listed.
local function note(names: {string}, seen: {[string]: boolean}, name: string): nil
    if seen[name] then
        return
    end
    seen[name] = true
    names[#names + 1] = name
end

--- Collects every group carrying a gain, a mute or a pause.
local function settledGroups(borrows self: Audio, names: {string}, seen: {[string]: boolean}): nil
    for name in pairs(self.groupGains) do
        note(names, seen, name)
    end
    for name in pairs(self.groupMutedFlags) do
        note(names, seen, name)
    end
    for name in pairs(self.groupPausedFlags) do
        note(names, seen, name)
    end
end

--- Returns every group this mixer knows about, sorted.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return a fresh sorted table the caller owns. A group whose only voice has
---     ended and which nothing was set on drops out of it, so this is not a
---     stable list of a game's groups.
function Audio.groups(borrows self: Audio): {string}
    local names: {string} = {}
    local seen: {[string]: boolean} = {}
    settledGroups(self, names, seen)
    for slot = 1, #self.voices do
        local voice = self.voices[slot]
        local group = voice.group
        if voice.sounding and group ~= nil then
            note(names, seen, group)
        end
    end
    table.sort(names)

    return names
end

--- Ends every voice in a group, over `fadeOut` seconds when that is given.
---
--- Deprecated: a group is a Bus; prefer `mixer:bus(name):track():stopAll`.
---
--- Stops the voices rather than the group: a gain, mute or pause set on the
--- name survives, and anything joining afterwards starts under them.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the group
--- @param name the group name
--- @param fadeOut seconds, a duration. Omitted or zero frees the slots within
---     this call, and a positive one unpauses the voices it fades.
function Audio.stopGroup(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, fadeOut: number?): nil
    local seconds = fadeOut or 0
    for slot = 1, #self.voices do
        local voice = self.voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding and voice.group == name then
            queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handleOf(self, slot)).fade = seconds > 0 and seconds or 0
            if seconds > 0 then
                voice.stopping = true
                voice.paused = false
            else
                release(self, slot)
            end
        end
    end
end

--- Caps how many voices a key may hold and how often it may start one.
---
--- A key is not a group. A group says where a sound's gain comes from and what
--- a pause reaches; a key says how many of one sound the mix will carry. The
--- two are set independently on `play`, so "at most three footsteps at once,
--- all of them in the effects group" is the ordinary case.
---
--- Reaching a limit drops the new voice: `play` returns zero and leaves every
--- sounding voice alone.
---
--- @param self the mixer holding the limit
--- @param key the bucket name
--- @param limit nil removes the limit. Lowering `voices` below what the key
---     already holds stops none of them and only refuses the next until enough
---     have ended. `cooldown` is measured against the time passed to `update`,
---     so a game that never calls `update` never advances one.
function Audio.setLimit(exclusive self: Audio, key: string, limit: Limit?): nil
    if limit == nil then
        self.limits[key] = nil
    else
        self.limits[key] = limit
    end
end

--- Returns the limit assigned to a key.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param key the bucket name
--- @return the record that was set rather than a copy, so writing through it
---     changes the limit in force. Nil when the key has none.
function Audio.limit(borrows self: Audio, key: string): Limit?
    return self.limits[key]
end

--- Returns how many voices a key holds now.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param key the bucket name
--- @return voices counted against the key, paused and fading ones included,
---     and zero for a key that has never held one
function Audio.keyCount(borrows self: Audio, key: string): integer
    local bucket = self.buckets[key]

    return bucket ~= nil and bucket.count or 0
end

--- Returns every key with a limit or a counted voice, sorted.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return a fresh table the caller owns. A key keeps its bucket once one
---     exists, so a key that has played and stopped is still listed.
function Audio.keys(borrows self: Audio): {string}
    local names: {string} = {}
    local seen: {[string]: boolean} = {}
    for name in pairs(self.limits) do
        note(names, seen, name)
    end
    for name in pairs(self.buckets) do
        note(names, seen, name)
    end
    table.sort(names)

    return names
end

--- Returns every sounding voice, paused and fading ones included.
---
--- For introspection, on the same terms as `clips`. The handles it reports are
--- the ones `playing` and `stop` take, so a caller can act on these results.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return a fresh list of fresh records each call, ordered by voice slot
---     rather than by when the voices started. It is a snapshot: nothing in it
---     follows the voice afterwards.
function Audio.voiceList(borrows self: Audio): {VoiceInfo}
    local list: {VoiceInfo} = {}
    for slot = 1, #self.voices do
        local voice = self.voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding then
            list[
                #list + 1
            ] = new VoiceInfo(
                handle = handleOf(self, slot),
                clip = clipPaths[voice.clip],
                gain = voice.gain,
                applied = voice.applied,
                pitch = voice.pitch,
                group = voice.group,
                key = voice.key,
                paused = voice.paused,
                stopping = voice.stopping,
                owned = voice.owned,
                loop = voice.loop,
                spatial = voice.spatial,
                x = voice.x,
                y = voice.y,
                z = voice.z,
                stereo = voice.stereo,
                left = voice.left,
                right = voice.right
            )
        end
    end

    return list
end

--- Logs what the output device did since the last report.
---
--- Warnings rather than information, because a game that went quiet is
--- diagnosed from these two lines and the default level is `"warn"`.
local function reportOutput(exclusive self: Audio): nil
    local status = self:output()
    if status.drops > self.outputDrops then
        LOGGER:warn("audio output dropped (%s); reopening it", status.reason or "no reason given")
    end
    if status.reopens > self.outputReopens then
        LOGGER:warn(
            "audio output reopened at %d Hz, %d channels; the mix stays at %d Hz, %d channels",
            status.frequency,
            status.channels,
            self.backend.frequency,
            self.backend.channels
        )
    end
    self.outputDrops = status.drops
    self.outputReopens = status.reopens
end

--- Sends queued commands and reaps the voices the backend has finished with.
---
--- Call once per frame with the frame's step. The step advances cooldowns, and
--- nothing else here needs time: a fade is the backend's to run, and a voice is
--- over when the backend says so rather than when a clock here says it should
--- be. It also advances every [`Player`](tecs.audio.sequencer.Player) made on this
--- mixer, which schedules on its voices' own clock rather than on the step.
---
--- Not a world system, and `install` deliberately does not add one: reaping has
--- to continue during a world pause, so the application drives it from each
--- iteration through `tick`, which skips it when the game already called it.
---
--- It also notices a dropped or reopened output device, logging each under
--- `tecs.audio`, and a terminal failure, after which `failureReason` is set.
---
--- @param self the mixer to advance
--- @param dt seconds since the last call. Omitting it advances nothing, which
---     is what a test stepping voices without time wants.
--- @return voices still sounding after the reap, the same number `sounding`
---     answers
function Audio.update(exclusive self: Audio, dt: number?): integer
    if self.closed then
        return 0
    end
    self.touched = true
    -- Music players first, so what they schedule this frame rides the same
    -- flush as the voices they start.
    local updates = audiosynth.updates[self]
    if updates ~= nil then
        local kept: integer = 0
        for index = 1, #updates do
            local advance = updates[index]
            if advance() then
                kept = kept + 1
                updates[kept] = advance
            end
        end
        for index = #updates, kept + 1, -1 do
            updates[index] = nil
        end
    end
    local changes = self.backend.outputChanges
    if changes ~= nil then
        local count = changes()
        if count ~= self.outputChanges then
            self.outputChanges = count
            reportOutput(self)
        end
    end
    local observe = self.backend.failure
    if self.failureReason == nil and observe ~= nil then
        local failure = observe()
        if failure ~= nil then
            self.failureReason = failure
            self.available = false
            -- Discard pending starts before issuing stops for every live handle.
            self.pendingCount = 0
            for slot = 1, #self.voices do
                if self.voices[slot].sounding then
                    queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handleOf(self, slot))
                    release(self, slot)
                end
            end
        end
    end
    self.elapsed = self.elapsed + (dt or 0)
    self:flush()

    local count = self.backend.drain(self.finished)
    for index = 1, count do
        local handle = self.finished[index]
        -- A handle whose slot has already been recycled fails the generation
        -- check, so a late report about a voice this mixer already released
        -- cannot take the voice that replaced it.
        local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
        if voice ~= nil then
            record(self, "finish", handle, nil, voice.clip, voice.group, nil)
            release(self, slotOf(handle))
        end
    end

    return self.live
end

--- Renders `seconds` of the mix on a memory output, whose clock moves only
--- here, then reaps what finished, as `update` does.
---
--- A frame fraction left over carries to the next call, so stepping by a
--- frame's `dt` sixty times renders exactly a second. Anything queued is sent
--- first, so a start just made sounds inside this step.
---
--- @param self the mixer to step
--- @param seconds how far to move the clock
--- @return the seconds rendered, and zero on any output but memory, whose
---     clock its device or the wall clock moves
--- @raises string when `seconds` is negative or not finite
function Audio.step(exclusive self: Audio, seconds: number): number
    if seconds ~= seconds or seconds < 0 or seconds == math.huge then
        error("tecs.audio.Audio.step: seconds must be finite and not negative", 2)
    end
    local render = self.backend.render
    local rate = self.backend.frequency
    if self.closed or render == nil or rate <= 0 then
        return 0
    end
    self:flush()
    local exact = seconds * rate + self.stepRemainder
    local frames = math.floor(exact)
    self.stepRemainder = exact - frames
    local done = render(frames)
    self:update(0)

    return done / rate
end

--- Holds the output clock so that only `step` moves it, or lets it run again.
---
--- A memory output is always held. A void output stops keeping time at the
--- wall clock's pace while held, and owes nothing for the hold when released,
--- which is what the debug harness does when it pauses and steps a frame at a
--- time. A device that is playing cannot be held.
---
--- @param self the mixer to hold
--- @param held true to hold, false to release
--- @return whether `step` now moves the clock
function Audio.hold(exclusive self: Audio, held: boolean): boolean
    local hold = self.backend.hold
    if self.closed or hold == nil then
        return false
    end
    self.stepRemainder = 0
    self.held = hold(held)

    return self.held
end

--- What the application calls once per iteration, paused or not: `update`,
--- unless the game already called it since the last tick, and on a held clock
--- a `step` of `dt`, so a memory output and a paused void output follow the
--- frames the application runs.
---
--- A game driving its own loop calls `update` itself and never needs this.
---
--- @param self the mixer to advance
--- @param dt the iteration's simulation step, zero for a held turn
function Audio.tick(exclusive self: Audio, dt: number): nil
    if self.closed then
        return
    end
    if self.held and dt > 0 then
        self:step(dt)
    elseif not self.touched then
        self:update(dt)
    end
    self.touched = false
end

--- Starts recording the next `seconds` of the mix, on any output: a memory
--- output records what `step` renders, a void output what its clock renders,
--- and a device what it is handed.
---
--- @param self the mixer to record
--- @param seconds how much to record; the recording stops filling there
--- @return whether the backend can record, which the silent one cannot
--- @raises string when `seconds` is not positive and finite
function Audio.listen(exclusive self: Audio, seconds: number): boolean
    if seconds ~= seconds or seconds <= 0 or seconds == math.huge then
        error("tecs.audio.Audio.listen: seconds must be positive and finite", 2)
    end
    local listen = self.backend.listen
    local rate = self.backend.frequency
    if self.closed or listen == nil or rate <= 0 then
        return false
    end
    self:flush()
    listen(math.ceil(seconds * rate))

    return true
end

--- Summarizes the recording `listen` started, as far as it has got.
---
--- @param self the mixer recording
--- @return the summary, or nil with no recording
function Audio.listening(borrows self: Audio): Listening?
    local summarize = self.backend.listenSummary
    if self.closed or summarize == nil then
        return nil
    end
    local text = summarize()
    if text == nil then
        return nil
    end
    local value = json.decode(text) as {[string]: any}?
    if value == nil then
        return nil
    end
    local started = self.backend.listenStarted

    return {
        frames = value.frames as integer,
        seconds = value.seconds as number,
        started = started and started() or 0,
        peak = value.peak as number,
        peakDbfs = value.peakDbfs as number?,
        rms = value.rms as number,
        rmsDbfs = value.rmsDbfs as number?,
        lufs = value.lufs as number?,
        clipped = value.clipped as integer,
        silent = value.silent as {{number}},
        onsets = value.onsets as {number}
    }
end

--- Writes the recording `listen` started, as far as it has got, to a 32-bit
--- float WAV file.
---
--- @param self the mixer recording
--- @param path where to write it
--- @return whether it was written
--- @return the reason when it was not
function Audio.saveListening(borrows self: Audio, path: string): (boolean, string?)
    local save = self.backend.listenSave
    if self.closed or save == nil then
        return false, "this backend cannot record"
    end

    return save(path)
end

--- Ends the recording `listen` started, freeing it.
---
--- @param self the mixer recording
function Audio.stopListening(exclusive self: Audio): nil
    local stop = self.backend.listenStop
    if not self.closed and stop ~= nil then
        stop()
    end
end

--- Returns the event log: starts, refusals, stops and finishes, each with its
--- time on the output clock, oldest first.
---
--- The log keeps the last 512 entries. A reader that remembers the
--- `sequence` of the last entry it saw passes it back to get only what came
--- after, so polling every frame costs only the new entries.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @param after the sequence of the last entry already seen, or nil for all
--- @return the entries, a fresh table the caller owns
function Audio.events(borrows self: Audio, after: integer?): {Event}
    local floor = after or 0
    local out: {Event} = {}
    local history = self.history
    for index = 1, #history do
        local entry = history[index]
        if entry.sequence > floor then
            out[#out + 1] = entry
        end
    end

    return out
end

--- Reports the counters an agent reads to see what the mixer refused or lost.
---
--- @param self the mixer to read
--- @return the counters, a fresh table
function Audio.stats(borrows self: Audio): Stats
    local late = self.backend.late
    local status = self:output()

    return {
        refused = self.refusals,
        steals = self.stolen,
        late = (late ~= nil and not self.closed) and late() or 0,
        drops = status.drops,
        reopens = status.reopens
    }
end

--- Returns the Bus with this name, making it under `options.parent` (Master
--- by default) when it does not exist. A group name is a Bus name: a voice
--- played with `group` sounds on that Bus's default Track.
---
--- @param self the mixer
--- @param name the Bus's name; `"master"` is Master
--- @param options applied when the Bus is made: parent, gain, mute, effects
--- @return the Bus
function Audio.bus(exclusive self: Audio, name: string, options: BusOptions?): Bus
    local found = self.busByName[name]
    if found ~= nil then
        return found
    end
    local parent = (options and options.parent) and self:bus(assert(options).parent as string)
        or assert(self.busByName[MASTER])
    local id = self.nextBus
    self.nextBus = id + 1
    stripCommand(self, audiobackend.BUS_CREATE, id, parent.id, 0, nil)
    local bus = newBus(self, name, id, parent.name)
    self.busByName[name] = bus
    self.busById[id] = bus
    local trackId = self.nextTrack
    self.nextTrack = trackId + 1
    stripCommand(self, audiobackend.TRACK_CREATE, trackId, id, 0, nil)
    local home = newTrack(self, trackId, name, 0)
    home.isDefault = true
    bus.home = home
    self.trackById[trackId] = home
    if options ~= nil then
        if options.gain ~= nil then
            bus:setGain(options.gain as number)
        end
        if options.muted == true then
            bus:setMuted(true)
        end
        for _, spec in ipairs(options.effects or {}) do
            bus:insert(spec)
        end
    end

    return bus
end

--- Returns Master.
function Audio.masterBus(exclusive self: Audio): Bus
    return assert(self.busByName[MASTER])
end

--- Returns every Bus, Master first and the rest by id, a fresh table.
function Audio.buses(borrows self: Audio): {Bus}
    local out: {Bus} = {}
    for id = 0, self.nextBus - 1 do
        local bus = self.busById[id]
        if bus ~= nil then
            out[#out + 1] = bus
        end
    end

    return out
end

--- Makes a Track: a persistent channel strip voices play into.
---
--- @param self the mixer
--- @param options bus, fader, mute, solo, position, sends, polyphony, effects
--- @return the Track
function Audio.track(exclusive self: Audio, options: TrackOptions?): Track
    local bus = (options and options.bus) and self:bus(assert(options).bus as string) or assert(self.busByName[MASTER])
    local id = self.nextTrack
    self.nextTrack = id + 1
    stripCommand(self, audiobackend.TRACK_CREATE, id, bus.id, 0, nil)
    local track = newTrack(self, id, bus.name, (options and options.polyphony) or self.defaultPolyphony)
    self.trackById[id] = track
    if options ~= nil then
        if options.gain ~= nil then
            track:setGain(options.gain as number)
        end
        if options.muted == true then
            track:setMuted(true)
        end
        if options.solo == true then
            track:setSolo(true)
        end
        if options.spatial == true then
            track:setPosition(options.x or 0, options.y or 0, options.z or 0)
        end
        for name, level in pairs(options.sends or {}) do
            track:send(name, level)
        end
        for _, spec in ipairs(options.effects or {}) do
            track:insert(spec)
        end
    end

    return track
end

--- Returns every Track, default ones included, by id, a fresh table.
function Audio.tracks(borrows self: Audio): {Track}
    local out: {Track} = {}
    for id = 0, self.nextTrack - 1 do
        local track = self.trackById[id]
        if track ~= nil then
            out[#out + 1] = track
        end
    end

    return out
end

--- Reads and resets every strip's meter: peak, RMS and clipped samples since
--- the last read, measured on the render path as it mixes.
---
--- @param self the mixer
--- @return Master, the Buses by name and the Tracks by id; all zero on a
---     backend that does not meter
function Audio.meters(exclusive self: Audio): Meters
    local silent: Meter = {peak = 0, rms = 0, clipped = 0}
    local out: Meters = {master = silent, buses = {}, tracks = {}}
    local read = self.backend.meters
    if self.closed or read == nil then
        return out
    end
    for _, reading in ipairs(read()) do
        local meter: Meter = {peak = reading.peak, rms = reading.rms, clipped = reading.clipped}
        if reading.bus then
            local bus = self.busById[reading.id]
            if reading.id == 0 then
                out.master = meter
            elseif bus ~= nil then
                out.buses[bus.name] = meter
            end
        elseif self.trackById[reading.id] ~= nil then
            out.tracks[reading.id] = meter
        end
    end

    return out
end

--- Makes a Timeline on this mixer's clock, which `update` advances.
---
--- @param self the mixer
--- @param options tempo, bar length, lookahead, start beat, paused
--- @return the timeline; `close` it to stop it
function Audio.timeline(exclusive self: Audio, options: timelines.Options?): timelines.Timeline
    local id = self.registry
    local made = timelines.new(
        function(): number
            return mixerOf(id):now()
        end,
        options,
        function(sound: any, given: any, _at: number): integer
            local mixer = mixerOf(id)

            return mixer:play(sound as Clip?, given as PlayOptions)
        end
    )
    local updates = audiosynth.updates[self]
    if updates == nil then
        updates = {}
        audiosynth.updates[self] = updates
    end
    updates[#updates + 1] = function(): boolean
        return made:update()
    end
    self.timelineList[#self.timelineList + 1] = made

    return made
end

--- Returns every Timeline made on this mixer and not closed, oldest first.
function Audio.timelines(borrows self: Audio): {timelines.Timeline}
    local out: {timelines.Timeline} = {}
    for _, line in ipairs(self.timelineList) do
        if not line:closedState() then
            out[#out + 1] = line
        end
    end

    return out
end

--- Returns the default Timeline, whose time is the clock: beat `n` is `n`
--- seconds on it, so "play this now" is an event at `now()`.
function Audio.defaultTimeline(exclusive self: Audio): timelines.Timeline
    local existing = self.clockline
    if existing ~= nil then
        return existing
    end
    local made = self:timeline({start = self:now()})
    self.clockline = made

    return made
end

--- Registers a Decoder an extension's library exports, given the address of
--- its vtable, and returns the id a Sound of that kind plays through.
---
--- @return the id, or nil and the reason the backend refused it
function Audio.registerDecoder(exclusive self: Audio, vtable: any): (integer?, string?)
    local register = self.backend.registerDecoder
    if self.closed or register == nil then
        return nil, "this backend takes no decoders"
    end

    return register(vtable)
end

--- Starts a Playback rendered by a registered Decoder, as `play` starts a
--- clip: the same slot, Track, gain, limits and steals.
---
--- The Decoder opens its instance now, on this thread, from `params` and
--- `bytes`, which mean what that Decoder says: a SoundFont id and a
--- polyphony for General MIDI, a chip and its memory images for FM.
---
--- @param self the mixer
--- @param decoder what `registerDecoder` returned; one is the built-in PCM
---     Decoder, which reads the bytes of an encoded file
--- @param params the Decoder's parameters
--- @param bytes the Sound's bytes, or empty
--- @param options as for `play`; `start`, `loop` and `loopStart` apply only
---     to a Decoder that seeks
--- @return the voice's handle, or zero and a reason
function Audio.playDecoded(
    exclusive self: Audio,
    decoder: integer,
    params: string,
    bytes: string,
    options: PlayOptions?
): (integer, string?)
    checkAt(options and options.at, "playDecoded")
    local open = self.backend.openDecoded
    local handle = claim(self, 0, audiobackend.SYNTH, options or {}, false)
    if handle == 0 then
        -- The refusal is the last entry the log took, with its reason.
        local last = self.history[#self.history]
        local why = last ~= nil and last.event == "refuse" and last.reason or nil
        local reasons: {
            [string]: string
        } = {
            unavailable = "the mixer has no output",
            limit = "the key's limit refused the start",
            voices = "every voice is busy",
            polyphony = "the Track's voices are all busy with higher priorities",
            track = "the Track has been destroyed"
        }

        return 0, why and reasons[why] or "the mixer refused the start"
    end
    if open == nil then
        -- A backend without Decoders takes the start and plays nothing, as
        -- the silent backend takes every other call.
        return handle
    end
    local opened, reason = open(decoder, handle, params, bytes)
    if not opened then
        self:stop(handle)

        return 0, reason
    end

    return handle
end

--- Sends a timed event to a voice's Decoder: a note, a register write,
--- whatever that Decoder's events mean. It lands on the frame `at` names, and
--- on the voice's first frame when it comes before the voice starts.
---
--- @param self the mixer
--- @param handle the voice
--- @param data the event, at most 2^53; tecs-audio-abi packs the common ones
--- @param at seconds on the output clock, or nil for at once
--- @return whether the handle names a sounding voice
--- @raises string for a time that is not finite
function Audio.event(exclusive self: Audio, handle: integer, data: integer, at: number?): boolean
    checkAt(at, "event")
    if voiceOf(self, handle) == nil then
        return false
    end
    local command = queue(self, audiobackend.EVENT, handle)
    command.data = data
    command.at = at or 0

    return true
end

--- Registers an Effect an extension's library exports, given the address of
--- its vtable, and returns the kind `tecs.audio.effects.custom` builds.
---
--- @return the kind, or nil and the reason the backend refused it
function Audio.registerEffect(exclusive self: Audio, vtable: any): (integer?, string?)
    local register = self.backend.registerEffect
    if self.closed or register == nil then
        return nil, "this backend takes no effects"
    end

    return register(vtable)
end

--- Options the audio pass fills per row, so a walk over a million sounds
--- allocates nothing.
local rowOptions: PlayOptions = {}

--- Puts the rows of disabled entities back to rest.
---
--- The walk below skips disabled rows, so the sweep treats their voices as
--- abandoned and takes them back. The row would still name a voice that has
--- gone, which reads on re-enabling as a one-shot that finished and never comes
--- back. Clearing the handle here is what makes the sound return with the
--- entity.
---
--- Archetype-shaped rather than per-row: a disabled entity sits in an archetype
--- of its own, so a world with nothing disabled matches nothing.
local function hush(hushed: ecs.Query): nil
    @unsafe do
        for source, length in hushed:iter() do
            local rows = source:get(SoundComponent)
            local total = length or 0
            if rows ~= nil then
                local mutable: Sound[?]? = nil
                for row = 1, total do
                    if rows[row].voice > 0 then
                        if mutable == nil then
                            mutable = source:getMut(SoundComponent)
                        end
                        -- Zero rather than finished: a voice the sweep is about to
                        -- take back is not one that ran to its end.
                        assert(mutable)[row].voice = 0
                    end
                end
            end
        end
    end
end

--- Starts, stops, follows and reaps the voices `Sound` components ask for.
---
--- One walk answers all of it. The pass starts a voice for a row asking to
--- sound, follows gain, rate, loop and position for sounding rows, returns
--- finished rows to rest, and treats a voice with no reference as belonging to
--- an entity that has gone or that the game disabled. That last case makes
--- despawning something mid-sound work without an observer on every archetype
--- that might hold one.
--- Sets an emitter Track's send to each zone's Bus by its distance from the
--- zone, changing only what moved.
local function sendToZones(exclusive self: Audio, strip: Track, x: number, y: number, z: number): nil
    local zones = self.zones
    local wanted: {[string]: number} = {}
    for _, zone in ipairs(zones) do
        local dx, dy, dz = x - zone.x, y - zone.y, z - zone.z
        local distance = math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz)
        if distance < zone.radius then
            local level = zone.level * (1 - distance / zone.radius)
            wanted[zone.bus] = math.max(wanted[zone.bus] or 0, level)
        end
    end
    local current = strip:sends()
    for bus, level in pairs(wanted) do
        if math.abs((current[bus] or 0) - level) > 1e-3 then
            strip:send(bus, level)
        end
    end
    for bus, _ in pairs(current) do
        if wanted[bus] == nil then
            strip:send(bus, 0)
        end
    end
end

--- The listener's position and scale, or nil without one.
local function listenerOf(listeners: {ecs.Query}): (number?, number?, number?, number?)
    @unsafe do
        for index, query in ipairs(listeners) do
            for source, length in query:iter() do
                if (length or 0) > 0 then
                    local found = assert(source:get(ListenerComponent))
                    local scale = found[1].scale
                    if index == 1 then
                        local places = assert(source:get(ecs.Transform2D))

                        return places[1].x, places[1].y, 0, scale
                    end
                    local places = assert(source:get(ecs.Transform3D))

                    return places[1].x, places[1].y, places[1].z, scale
                end
            end
        end
    end

    return nil, nil, nil, nil
end

--- Places spatial rows from their transforms against the listener, and
--- gathers the zones, before the rows are reconciled.
local function listen(
    exclusive self: Audio,
    listeners: {ecs.Query},
    emitters: {ecs.Query},
    zoneQueries: {ecs.Query}
): nil
    local foundX, foundY, foundZ, scale = listenerOf(listeners)
    self.zones = {}
    if foundX == nil then
        return
    end
    local lx: number = foundX
    local ly: number = foundY or 0
    local lz: number = foundZ or 0
    local unit = (scale ~= nil and scale > 0) and scale or 1
    @unsafe do
        for index, query in ipairs(emitters) do
            local flat = index == 1
            for source, length in query:iter() do
                local rows = source:get(SoundComponent)
                local total = length or 0
                if rows ~= nil then
                    local mutable: Sound[?]? = nil
                    for row = 1, total do
                        local entry = rows[row]
                        if entry.spatial then
                            local x: number = 0
                            local y: number = 0
                            local z: number = 0
                            if flat then
                                local places = assert(source:get(ecs.Transform2D))
                                x = (places[row].x - lx) / unit
                                y = -(places[row].y - ly) / unit
                            else
                                local places = assert(source:get(ecs.Transform3D))
                                x = (places[row].x - lx) / unit
                                y = (places[row].y - ly) / unit
                                z = (places[row].z - lz) / unit
                            end
                            if x ~= entry.x or y ~= entry.y or z ~= entry.z then
                                if mutable == nil then
                                    mutable = source:getMut(SoundComponent)
                                end
                                local written = assert(mutable)[row]
                                written.x = x
                                written.y = y
                                written.z = z
                            end
                        end
                    end
                end
            end
        end
        for index, query in ipairs(zoneQueries) do
            local flat = index == 1
            for source, length in query:iter() do
                local zones = assert(source:get(ReverbZoneComponent))
                for row = 1, (length or 0) do
                    local zone = zones[row]
                    local bus = groupNames[zone.bus]
                    if bus ~= nil then
                        local x: number = 0
                        local y: number = 0
                        local z: number = 0
                        if flat then
                            local places = assert(source:get(ecs.Transform2D))
                            x = (places[row].x - lx) / unit
                            y = -(places[row].y - ly) / unit
                        else
                            local places = assert(source:get(ecs.Transform3D))
                            x = (places[row].x - lx) / unit
                            y = (places[row].y - ly) / unit
                            z = (places[row].z - lz) / unit
                        end
                        self.zones[
                            #self.zones + 1
                        ] = new Zone(bus = bus, x = x, y = y, z = z, radius = zone.radius / unit, level = zone.level)
                    end
                end
            end
        end
    end
end

local function reconcile(exclusive self: Audio, sounds: ecs.Query, hushed: ecs.Query): nil
    local voices = self.voices
    for slot = 1, #voices do
        voices[slot].referenced = false
    end

    hush(hushed)

    @unsafe do
        for source, length in sounds:iter() do
            local rows = source:get(SoundComponent)
            local total = length or 0
            if rows ~= nil then
                -- Taken mutable on the first row that actually changes and not at
                -- all otherwise, because taking it up front would mark the column
                -- dirty every frame for every archetype holding a sound.
                local mutable: Sound[?]? = nil

                for row = 1, total do
                    local entry = rows[row]
                    local handle = entry.voice

                    if not entry.playing then
                        -- Asked for silence. The row goes back to zero rather than
                        -- to finished, so setting the flag again starts it: a
                        -- one-shot that has run out plays a second time instead of
                        -- staying spent.
                        if handle ~= 0 then
                            if voiceOf(self, handle) ~= nil then
                                queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handle)
                                release(self, slotOf(handle))
                            end
                            if mutable == nil then
                                mutable = source:getMut(SoundComponent)
                            end
                            assert(mutable)[row].voice = 0
                        end
                    elseif handle == 0 then
                        rowOptions.gain = entry.gain
                        rowOptions.loop = entry.loop
                        rowOptions.pitch = entry.pitch
                        rowOptions.spatial = entry.spatial
                        rowOptions.x = entry.x
                        rowOptions.y = entry.y
                        rowOptions.z = entry.z
                        -- Nil for index zero, which is what "no group" reads as.
                        -- Only rows that are starting pay for the lookup: a
                        -- sounding voice keeps the group it joined.
                        rowOptions.group = groupNames[entry.group]
                        -- A spatial row is an emitter: a Track of its own,
                        -- placed and sent to its zones, rather than a
                        -- placement on its voice.
                        local strip: Track? = nil
                        rowOptions.track = nil
                        if entry.spatial then
                            local made = self:track({bus = rowOptions.group, polyphony = 0})
                            made:setPosition(entry.x, entry.y, entry.z)
                            rowOptions.spatial = false
                            rowOptions.track = made
                            strip = made
                        end
                        local started = start(self, self.clipsById[entry.clip], rowOptions, true)
                        if started ~= 0 then
                            if mutable == nil then
                                mutable = source:getMut(SoundComponent)
                            end
                            assert(mutable)[row].voice = started
                            local voice = voices[slotOf(started)]
                            voice.referenced = true
                            voice.strip = strip
                            if strip ~= nil then
                                sendToZones(self, strip, entry.x, entry.y, entry.z)
                            end
                        elseif strip ~= nil then
                            strip:destroy()
                        end
                    else
                        local voice = voiceOf(self, handle)
                        if voice == nil then
                            if mutable == nil then
                                mutable = source:getMut(SoundComponent)
                            end
                            assert(mutable)[row].voice = FINISHED
                        else
                            voice.referenced = true
                            local slot = slotOf(handle)
                            -- Each of these is a load and a compare, and the call
                            -- past it runs only on the frame the value moved.
                            if voice.gain ~= entry.gain then
                                voice.gain = entry.gain
                                applyGain(self, slot)
                            end
                            if voice.pitch ~= entry.pitch then
                                applyPitch(self, slot, entry.pitch)
                            end
                            if voice.loop ~= entry.loop then
                                applyLoop(self, slot, entry.loop)
                            end
                            local strip = voice.strip
                            if strip ~= nil then
                                local x, y, z = strip:position()
                                if x ~= entry.x or y ~= entry.y or z ~= entry.z then
                                    strip:setPosition(entry.x, entry.y, entry.z)
                                end
                                sendToZones(self, strip, entry.x, entry.y, entry.z)
                            elseif entry.spatial then
                                applyPosition(self, slot, entry.x, entry.y, entry.z)
                            elseif voice.spatial then
                                applyUnplaced(self, slot)
                            end
                        end
                    end
                end
            end
        end
    end

    for slot = 1, #voices do
        local voice = voices[slot]
        if voice.sounding and voice.owned and not voice.referenced then
            queue(self, audiobackend.STOP, handleOf(self, slot))
            release(self, slot)
        end
    end

    self:flush()
end

--- Returns the mixer state a snapshot carries.
---
--- The master gain and mute, and every group's gain, mute and pause. A player
--- who turned the music down and held the effects for a menu expects to find it
--- that way after a load, and none of it is in the world.
---
--- Keyed limits are not here, and neither is what a bucket holds. A limit is a
--- rule the build states at startup beside the clip it governs, so persisted
--- data must not override it. Voices determine each bucket, and a snapshot
--- restores no voices.
local function saveMixer(borrows self: Audio): any
    local groups: {[string]: any} = {}
    for _, name in ipairs(self:groups()) do
        local gain = self.groupGains[name]
        local muted = self.groupMutedFlags[name] == true
        local paused = self.groupPausedFlags[name] == true
        -- A group that only has voices in it has nothing set, and writing its
        -- defaults out would make a save grow with what a run happened to play.
        if gain ~= nil or muted or paused then
            groups[name] = {gain = gain or 1, muted = muted, paused = paused}
        end
    end

    -- These saved keys are a compatibility surface.

    return {masterGain = self.master, muted = self.mutedFlag, groups = groups}
end

--- Puts saved mixer state back, through the ordinary setters.
---
--- Visits every group this mixer knows about rather than only the ones the
--- snapshot names, so an omitted group returns to its defaults instead of
--- keeping this run's settings. Going through the setters makes sounding voices
--- follow.
local function loadMixer(exclusive self: Audio, value: any): nil
    local saved = value as {[string]: any}?
    local savedGroups = saved ~= nil and saved.groups as {[string]: any}? or nil

    local names: {string} = {}
    local seen: {[string]: boolean} = {}
    settledGroups(self, names, seen)
    if savedGroups ~= nil then
        for name in pairs(savedGroups) do
            note(names, seen, name)
        end
    end

    for _, name in ipairs(names) do
        local entry = savedGroups ~= nil and savedGroups[name] as {[string]: any}? or nil
        self:setGroupGain(name, entry ~= nil and (entry.gain as number?) or 1)
        self:setGroupMuted(name, entry ~= nil and entry.muted == true)
        if entry ~= nil and entry.paused == true then
            self:pauseGroup(name)
        else
            self:resumeGroup(name)
        end
    end

    self:setMasterGain(saved ~= nil and (saved.masterGain as number?) or 1)
    self:setMuted(saved ~= nil and saved.muted == true)
end

--- Returns the mixer installed into a world.
---
--- What lets something holding only the world reach the mixer, which is what
--- the debug tools have and what a game writing its own systems often has too.
---
--- @param world the world to inspect
--- @return the mixer, or nil before `install` and again after `destroy`
export function of(borrows world: ecs.World): Audio?
    return installed[world]
end

--- Creates a mixer, installs it into a world, and returns it.
---
--- Adds the system that plays [`Sound`](tecs.audio.Sound) components and the
--- snapshot handler that carries master and group settings.
--- Pitch variance uses the world's named tecs.audio random stream, so reseeding
--- and snapshot restore also restart future pitch draws. Standalone mixers made
--- with newAudio keep an independent Nupp generator.
---
--- `update` is not added here. Reaping voices is not world work: it has to
--- continue during a world pause, so the application drives it from each
--- iteration through `tick`.
---
--- @param world the world to install into, which expects one mixer
--- @param config the mixer settings, or nil for the defaults
--- @return the new mixer, also available through `of`
--- @raises string when the world already has a mixer installed
export function install(exclusive world: ecs.World, config: Config?): Audio
    if installed[world] ~= nil then
        error("tecs.audio.install: the world already has a mixer installed", 2)
    end
    local mixer = newAudio(config)
    -- This stream name is a compatibility surface from the old world registry.
    mixer.variance = world:randomStream("tecs.audio")
    installed[world] = mixer

    local sounds = world:newQuery({include = {SoundComponent}, exclude = {ecs.Disabled}})
    -- The disabled rows and only those. They sit in archetypes of their own, so
    -- this matches nothing until something is disabled.
    local hushed = world:newQuery({include = {SoundComponent, ecs.Disabled}})

    local listeners: {ecs.Query} = {
        world:newQuery({
            include = {ListenerComponent, ecs.Transform2D}
        }),
        world:newQuery({
            include = {ListenerComponent, ecs.Transform3D}
        }),
    }
    local emitters: {ecs.Query} = {
        world:newQuery({
            include = {SoundComponent, ecs.Transform2D},
            exclude = {ecs.Disabled}
        }),
        world:newQuery({include = {SoundComponent, ecs.Transform3D}, exclude = {ecs.Disabled}}),
    }
    local zoneQueries: {ecs.Query} = {
        world:newQuery({
            include = {ReverbZoneComponent, ecs.Transform2D},
            exclude = {ecs.Disabled}
        }),
        world:newQuery({include = {ReverbZoneComponent, ecs.Transform3D}, exclude = {ecs.Disabled}}),
    }
    mixer.playSystem = function(_dt: number, exclusive _world: ecs.World): nil
        listen(mixer, listeners, emitters, zoneQueries)
        reconcile(mixer, sounds, hushed)
    end

    world:addSystem({
        -- This system name is a compatibility surface: the debug server reports
        -- it and an agent selects on it.
        name = "tecs.PlaySounds",
        -- After gameplay has decided what exists and before anything renders,
        -- so a sound spawned this frame starts this frame.
        phase = ecs.phases.PostUpdate,
        run = assert(mixer.playSystem),
    })

    world:addSnapshotHandler({
        -- This snapshot handler key is a compatibility surface: a save file
        -- written by an earlier build names it.
        name = "tecs.audio",
        save = function(exclusive _world: ecs.World): any
            return saveMixer(mixer)
        end,
        load = function(exclusive _world: ecs.World, value: any): nil
            loadMixer(mixer, value)
        end,
    })

    return mixer
end

--- The native device provider, resolved once and kept.
---
--- Resolving it loads `libtecsaudio` and builds a record of closures. It opens
--- nothing and lists nothing, so this is safe to do on a machine with no sound
--- and safe to do before a game has decided whether it wants any.
local resolvedDevices: {audiobackend.Devices} = {}

--- Returns the provider a call should use.
---
--- @param given the provider a caller supplied, or nil to resolve the native one
--- @return the caller's provider, the native one, or the silent fallback
local function providerFor(given: audiobackend.Devices?): audiobackend.Devices
    if given ~= nil then
        return given
    end
    local existing = resolvedDevices[1]
    if existing ~= nil then
        return existing
    end
    local resolved: audiobackend.Devices? = nil
    local loaded, native = pcall(require, "tecs.platform.audionative")
    if loaded then
        resolved = (native as {[string]: any}).devices() as audiobackend.Devices?
    else
        LOGGER:info("no audio devices: %s", tostring(native))
    end
    if resolved == nil then
        resolved = audiobackend.silentDevices()
    end
    resolvedDevices[1] = resolved

    return resolved
end

--- Names the playback devices attached now.
---
--- A snapshot rather than a subscription: devices come and go while a game
--- runs, so an id held across a hotplug may name nothing. A
--- [`Device`](tecs.audio.Device) `name` is what survives a run.
---
--- @param devices the provider to ask, or nil for the platform's own
--- @return the devices, listed afresh each call and the caller's to keep. Empty
---     rather than nil when nothing could be asked, so a caller that only
---     iterates needs no nil check.
--- @return the reason, when something went wrong. Nil on success, including for
---     a machine that genuinely has no playback device.
export function playbackDevices(devices: audiobackend.Devices?): ({Device}, string?)
    return providerFor(devices).playback()
end

--- Names the recording devices attached now.
---
--- @param devices the provider to ask, or nil for the platform's own
--- @return the devices, read as `playbackDevices` reads its own
--- @return the reason, when something went wrong
export function recordingDevices(devices: audiobackend.Devices?): ({Device}, string?)
    return providerFor(devices).recording()
end

--- Opens a microphone as interleaved native-endian 32-bit float samples.
---
--- No callback is installed anywhere. The backend's own thread fills a bounded
--- buffer and the game pulls completed frames from the frame thread with
--- [`Microphone.read`](tecs.audio.Microphone), because a device thread is one
--- the Lua virtual machine never created and entering Nupp from it is
--- undefined.
---
--- @param config the settings, or nil to open the system's current default
---     recording device at 48000 Hz in mono with a one-second buffer
--- @return an open microphone, already recording: capture is running before
---     this returns, so frames accumulate from here whether or not anything
---     reads them. Nil on failure, with the reason beside it, and nothing is
---     left open in that case.
--- @return the reason, when the first return is nil
--- @raises string when `frequency` is not a positive integer, when `channels` is not
---     an integer from 1 to 32, or when `bufferFrames` is not a positive
---     integer
export function openMicrophone(config: MicrophoneConfig?): (Microphone?, string?)
    local frequency = (config and config.frequency) or DEFAULT_CAPTURE_FREQUENCY
    local channels = (config and config.channels) or DEFAULT_CAPTURE_CHANNELS
    if frequency < 1 then
        -- These two refusals are compatibility surfaces: the SDL implementation
        -- published them word for word, so the wording stays even though the
        -- house rule moved them from a returned reason to a raise. Neither
        -- checks that the value is whole any more, because `integer` is its own
        -- type here and a fractional one is refused before the call runs.
        error("tecs.audio.openMicrophone: microphone frequency must be a positive integer", 2)
    end
    if channels < 1 or channels > MAX_CAPTURE_CHANNELS then
        error("tecs.audio.openMicrophone: microphone channels must be an integer from 1 to 32", 2)
    end
    local bufferFrames = (config and config.bufferFrames) or frequency
    if bufferFrames < 1 then
        error("tecs.audio.openMicrophone: bufferFrames must be a positive integer", 2)
    end

    local provider = providerFor(config and config.devices)
    local capture, reason = provider.open(
        (config and config.device) or 0,
        config and config.deviceName,
        frequency,
        channels,
        bufferFrames
    )
    if capture == nil then
        return nil, reason or "no recording device opened"
    end

    return new Microphone(frequency = capture.frequency, channels = capture.channels, capture = capture)
end

function Microphone.failure(borrows self: Microphone): string?
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        return "microphone is destroyed"
    end
    local observe = capture.failure

    return observe ~= nil and observe() or nil
end

function Microphone.availableFrames(borrows self: Microphone): integer
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        return 0
    end

    return capture.availableFrames()
end

function Microphone.overruns(borrows self: Microphone): integer
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        return 0
    end

    return capture.overruns()
end

--- Returns how many frames a call should take, given what is ready.
---
--- @param ready the frames the capture has
--- @param maxFrames the caller's ceiling, or nil for everything ready
--- @return the frames to take
--- @raises string when `maxFrames` is not a non-negative integer
local function capped(ready: integer, maxFrames: integer?): integer
    if maxFrames == nil then
        return ready
    end
    if maxFrames < 0 then
        -- Another compatibility surface, kept word for word. The SDL
        -- implementation also had to check that the value was whole, and this
        -- one does not: `integer` is its own type here, so a fractional count
        -- is refused before the call runs.
        error("maxFrames must be a non-negative integer", 3)
    end
    if maxFrames < ready then
        return maxFrames
    end

    return ready
end

function Microphone.read(exclusive self: Microphone, maxFrames: integer?): (string?, string?)
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        -- This reason is a compatibility surface: the SDL implementation
        -- returned it word for word.
        return nil, "microphone is destroyed"
    end
    local frames = capped(capture.availableFrames(), maxFrames)
    if frames == 0 then
        local failure = self:failure()
        if maxFrames ~= 0 and failure ~= nil then
            return nil, failure
        end
        return ""
    end

    return capture.read(frames)
end

function Microphone.readInto(exclusive self: Microphone, out: {number}, maxFrames: integer?): integer
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        return 0
    end
    local frames = capped(capture.availableFrames(), maxFrames)
    if frames == 0 then
        local failure = self:failure()
        if maxFrames ~= 0 and failure ~= nil then
            error(failure, 2)
        end
        return 0
    end

    return capture.readInto(out, frames)
end

function Microphone.pause(exclusive self: Microphone): (boolean, string?)
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        return false, "microphone is destroyed"
    end

    return capture.pause()
end

function Microphone.resume(exclusive self: Microphone): (boolean, string?)
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        return false, "microphone is destroyed"
    end

    local failure = self:failure()
    if failure ~= nil then
        return false, failure
    end

    return capture.resume()
end

function Microphone.destroy(exclusive self: Microphone): nil
    local capture = self.capture
    if capture == nil then
        return
    end
    self.capture = nil
    capture.close()
end
